---
title: Takeover & Detached Runs (Advanced)
id: sandbox-takeover
order: 13
description: "Let a sandboxed agent run outlive the browser tab: detach on disconnect, then take the run over from another host and resume streaming where the log left off."
keywords:
  - detached run
  - run takeover
  - sandboxRunDriver
  - detachOnDisconnect
  - detachedRunTtlMs
  - requestRunCancel
  - JournalReplayDivergedError
  - driverEpoch
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A sandboxed coding agent can work for ten minutes. A browser tab does not last\nten minutes. Users refresh, close the laptop, lose wifi, and hit a load balancer\nthat lands the next request on a different replica."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Without durability wired, the disconnect is fatal: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":"'s abort path\ndestroys the sandbox on every abort, deliberately, because closing the agent's\nIO stream does "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" kill the agent process (a Docker "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exec"},{"type":"text","value":" survives its\nclient), so destroying the container is the only reliable way to stop it burning\ntokens. Correct for a cancel. Ruinous for a refresh."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable runs split those two cases apart. A disconnect "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"detaches"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the agent\nkeeps working, the sandbox stays up, and the run record remembers that nobody is\nwatching. A later request "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"takes the run over"}]},{"type":"text","value":", replays what was already\ndelivered, and continues streaming the rest."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This page is the wiring. It builds on two pages you should read first:\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./journal","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Run Journal"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (where the agent's output lives, and why it is a\nfile rather than a pipe) and\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../resumable-streams/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resumable Streams"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (the delivery log a client\nreconnects to)."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"durability-is-one-opt-in-not-two","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durability is one opt-in, not two"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" takes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"},{"type":"text","value":". A run is durable only when "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"both"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nare present, because either alone is useless: a record with no event log cannot\nbe replayed, and a log with no record cannot be found, claimed, or reaped. There\nis no half-configured state. Pass one and you get exactly today's behavior,\nsilently and with no warning, because you have not asked for durability."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"same"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":" chat persistence uses, so one record describes the\nrun instead of two that disagree."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"both-routes-must-address-the-same-backend-stream","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both routes must address the same backend stream"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read this before the snippets. A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamDurability"},{"type":"text","value":" is bound to "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"one run"}]},{"type":"text","value":",\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream(request, options)"},{"type":"text","value":" resolves which run through core's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveResumeRunId"},{"type":"text","value":": the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"X-Run-Id"},{"type":"text","value":" header first, then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"?runId"},{"type":"text","value":" on the\nrequest URL. Core's own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream"},{"type":"text","value":" uses the same resolver, so no two\ndurability adapters can disagree about which run a request names."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"POST from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-client"}]},{"type":"text","value":" carries the run id in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"X-Run-Id"},{"type":"text","value":"\nheader, not the URL. The POST URL stays byte-identical to a plain, non-durable\nchat request."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"GET join"}]},{"type":"text","value":" carries it in the URL instead: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"joinRun"},{"type":"text","value":" requests\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"?offset=-1&runId=<runId>"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"So "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream(request, durableOptions)"},{"type":"text","value":" on both routes resolves the same\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"agent-runs/<runId>"},{"type":"text","value":" stream, whichever way the run id arrived. Nothing to rewrite,\nnothing to force onto a copy of the URL:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\n\n// The external Durable Streams backend every replica can reach. See\n// ../resumable-streams/advanced for the full option set (auth headers, batch\n// size, reconnect tuning).\nexport const durableOptions = {\n  server: 'https://streams.example.com',\n  streamPrefix: 'agent-runs',\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A request naming no run at all (neither header nor query) throws rather than\nsilently producing into a stream no attach request could ever name:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"durableStream: a runId is required: send it as an X-Run-Id header or a\n?runId query param"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"That only bites a client that bypasses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-client"},{"type":"text","value":" (a custom\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetch"},{"type":"text","value":", a hand-rolled reconnect): make sure it sends one of the two, an\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"X-Run-Id"},{"type":"text","value":" header on the POST or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"?runId"},{"type":"text","value":" on the URL. A mid-stream SSE\nreconnect to the POST route still works. It arrives carrying "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Last-Event-ID"},{"type":"text","value":" and the\nsame "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"X-Run-Id"},{"type":"text","value":" header the original POST used, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream"},{"type":"text","value":" resolves the run and\nhonors the resume offset."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"And a caller with no live "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Request"},{"type":"text","value":" at all (a cron, a Durable Object "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alarm()"},{"type":"text","value":")\nsynthesizes one from scratch instead. See the reaper's own\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durabilityFor"},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"link","href":"./reaping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reaping & Retention"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"server-start-a-durable-run","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server: start a durable run"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { withLocks } from '@tanstack/ai/locks'\nimport { claudeCodeText } from '@tanstack/ai-claude-code'\nimport { memoryPersistence, withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\n// The options from the section above.\nimport { durableOptions } from './durability'\n// Your distributed LockStore. `InMemoryLockStore` is NOT enough here. See\n// \"Requirements\" below.\nimport { locks } from './locks'\n// Your `defineSandbox(...)` result.\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\n\n// Development stand-in. A durable run needs a store every replica can read;\n// see ../persistence/build-your-own-adapter.\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\nconst { runs } = persistence.stores\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  // ONE adapter instance, handed to both the middleware and the transport, so\n  // the journal path and the delivery log describe the same run.\n  // `@tanstack/ai-client` sends `X-Run-Id` on every POST, which is exactly what\n  // `durableStream` resolves first, so this already addresses\n  // `agent-runs/<runId>`: the same stream the attach route below reads.\n  const adapter = durableStream(request, durableOptions)\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: claudeCodeText('claude-opus-4-8'),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    // Required for a durable run. Omit it and `chatStream` throws\n    // `DurableRunIdRequiredError`. See \"Requirements\".\n    runId,\n    middleware: [\n      withPersistence(persistence),\n      withLocks(locks),\n      withSandbox(sandbox, {\n        runs,\n        durability: { adapter },\n      }),\n    ],\n  })\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, { durability: { adapter } })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Three things changed compared with a plain sandboxed chat endpoint, and each one\nis load bearing:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"}]},{"type":"text","value":" turn on detach-on-disconnect and publish\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DetachableRunCapability"},{"type":"text","value":" on the bus, which is how "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" learns\nthat an abort on this run is a detach rather than a cancel."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":" is forwarded"}]},{"type":"text","value":", not generated. The journal path, the deterministic\nmessage-id generator, "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"and the backend stream name"}]},{"type":"text","value":" are all derived from it,\nso a successor host can only resume a run whose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":" it can recompute."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapter instance is shared"}]},{"type":"text","value":" between "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" and the response, and it is\nkeyed by that same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":". That is what makes the attach route below address this\nvery stream."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One thing must "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" be there: an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"abortController"},{"type":"text","value":" that mirrors "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"request.signal"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A plain sandboxed endpoint mirrors it, because there a disconnect should end the run.\nOn a durable run it destroys the thing you are trying to protect. Aborting the run\nmakes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" return at its cancellation check right after middleware "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setup"},{"type":"text","value":", so the\nharness adapter's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chatStream"},{"type":"text","value":" is never called and "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the agent inside the sandbox you\njust spent minutes building is never launched"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Switch tabs while the UI still says\n\"starting the sandbox\" and you come back to an empty log for a run that did nothing.\nNo takeover can recover it either, because an agent that never started wrote no\njournal to replay."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The disconnect still reaches "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" without it. The durable transport notifies\nthe run the moment the response body is cancelled, "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"without"}]},{"type":"text","value":" aborting it, and then:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" stamps "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedSince"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxKey"},{"type":"text","value":", and publishes the detach\nverdict."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The run keeps draining into its still-open delivery log."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A rejoining client tails that log."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Passing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"},{"type":"text","value":" buys all of that. There is nothing else to wire."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A genuine stop is unaffected, because it arrives out of band (see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"#detach-vs-cancel","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Detach vs cancel"}]},{"type":"text","value":"). That is the only channel that can tell \"the\nuser wants this stopped\" from \"the user closed a tab\", since both are the same socket\nclose on the wire."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"on-memorystream-raise-the-first-chunk-deadline","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream"},{"type":"text","value":", raise the first-chunk deadline"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A rejoin during the sandbox build fails with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Memory stream run produced no data within 100ms"},{"type":"text","value":", and the run it calls gone is\nhealthy. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream"},{"type":"text","value":" gives up on a from-start rejoin when the run produces no chunk\nwithin "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"firstChunkDeadlineMs"},{"type":"text","value":", which defaults to 100ms. That default fits chat, where\nan in-flight run's log already holds chunks. A sandboxed run emits nothing until\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ensure"},{"type":"text","value":" has built the sandbox and cloned the repo."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Raise it on "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"every"}]},{"type":"text","value":" handle for the run. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" that serves the rejoin is the call\nthat actually applies it:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { memoryStream } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\n// Longer than your slowest `ensure`.\nconst FIRST_CHUNK_DEADLINE_MS = 15 * 60_000;\n\n// POST (the producer) and GET (the rejoin) alike.\nexport function adapterFor(request: Request) {\n  return memoryStream(request, {\n    firstChunkDeadlineMs: FIRST_CHUNK_DEADLINE_MS,\n  });\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Failing fast buys nothing once you gate the rejoin on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"findActiveRun"},{"type":"text","value":", which already\nexcludes a run that is really gone before the join is attempted."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two things make this a backstop rather than the main defence:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream"},{"type":"text","value":" runs no first-chunk deadline at all. Its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"read"},{"type":"text","value":" parks for a live\nreader, so an empty in-flight log simply waits."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A fresh durable producer appends one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CUSTOM"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"run.accepted"},{"type":"text","value":" chunk\n("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ACCEPTED_EVENT"},{"type":"text","value":") before it pulls the producer stream, so a rejoin attaches in\nmilliseconds instead of waiting on the harness. That chunk also keeps the client from\nabandoning the join, since "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ai-client"},{"type":"text","value":" gives up on a rejoin that receives nothing for\n2s and a sandbox build always takes longer."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"server-take-the-run-over","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server: take the run over"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The takeover happens in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" handler that already serves resumes. Add a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"driver"},{"type":"text","value":" and the same request that replays the log also claims the run and keeps\ndriving it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every adapter here comes from the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"same"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream(request, durableOptions)"},{"type":"text","value":"\nthe "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"POST"},{"type":"text","value":" route used, so the log this route replays, the log the drive appends\nto, and the log the producing route wrote are provably one stream:\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"agent-runs/<runId>"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, resumeServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { withLocks } from '@tanstack/ai/locks'\nimport { claudeCodeText } from '@tanstack/ai-claude-code'\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\nimport { memoryPersistence, withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { sandboxRunDriver, withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n// The same backend options as the POST route.\nimport { durableOptions } from './durability'\nimport { locks } from './locks'\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\nimport type { StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\nconst { messages: messageStore, runs } = persistence.stores\n\n/**\n * The claim hands `drive` an `AbortSignal` that fires the moment this host loses\n * ownership; `chat()` takes an `AbortController`. Mirror one onto the other so a\n * lost claim actually stops the drive.\n */\nfunction controllerFor(signal: AbortSignal): AbortController {\n  const controller = new AbortController()\n  const abort = (): void => controller.abort(signal.reason)\n  if (signal.aborted) abort()\n  else signal.addEventListener('abort', abort, { once: true })\n  return controller\n}\n\nexport function GET(request: Request) {\n  async function* driveRun(input: {\n    runId: string\n    threadId: string\n    signal: AbortSignal\n  }): AsyncIterable<StreamChunk> {\n    // The client sent no history: it is reconnecting, not asking a question.\n    const stored = await messageStore.loadThread(input.threadId)\n    const stream = chat({\n      adapter: claudeCodeText('claude-opus-4-8'),\n      messages: stored,\n      threadId: input.threadId,\n      runId: input.runId,\n      abortController: controllerFor(input.signal),\n      middleware: [\n        withPersistence(persistence),\n        withLocks(locks),\n        withSandbox(sandbox, {\n          runs,\n          // `attach: true` is the whole difference: the harness tails the run's\n          // EXISTING journal instead of starting a second agent. It belongs\n          // here and never on `chat()`, which has no sandbox vocabulary. It\n          // is set only by an attach route, never by a POST handler.\n          durability: {\n            // `request` already names this run (`?runId` on an attach GET),\n            // so `durableStream` resolves the same `agent-runs/<runId>` the\n            // journal replay aligns against.\n            adapter: durableStream(request, durableOptions),\n            attach: true,\n          },\n        }),\n      ],\n    })\n    for await (const chunk of stream) yield chunk\n  }\n\n  return resumeServerSentEventsResponse({\n    // The replaying adapter is the one adapter here whose `resumeFrom()` matters,\n    // and the offset it must return (`?offset=-1` from a join, or an SSE\n    // reconnect's `Last-Event-ID`) lives on the incoming request. `durableStream`\n    // resolves the run the same way on every route, `X-Run-Id` header first,\n    // then `?runId`, so this addresses the same `agent-runs/<runId>` the POST\n    // route wrote, whichever way a given request names the run.\n    adapter: durableStream(request, durableOptions),\n    driver: sandboxRunDriver({\n      request,\n      runs,\n      locks,\n      // Per-run log factory. Core resolves the id from this same request\n      // through the same `resolveResumeRunId` `durableStream` uses, so every\n      // call for this run talks to the same backend stream and `snapshot()`\n      // sees this host's own appends. The state lives in the Durable Streams\n      // backend, not this process.\n      durability: () => durableStream(request, durableOptions),\n      drive: driveRun,\n    }),\n  })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The response is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"byte-identical"}]},{"type":"text","value":" whether or not you pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"driver"},{"type":"text","value":": it still\nreplays from the durability log. The drive runs beside it, appending to the run's\nproducer-side log, and the response tails what lands. That separation is what\nlets a taken-over run keep "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":"'s normal middleware path, so\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":"'s "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFinish"},{"type":"text","value":" still saves the transcript of a run that finished\nwhile detached."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Everything about the takeover is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"total by construction"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Every failure is\nlogged and swallowed, and the response still serves the log:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Situation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What happens"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No run id on the request, or no record"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Serve the log, drive nothing."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The record is already terminal"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Serve the log, drive nothing. A second tab attaching to a finished run must still see the transcript."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Another host holds the claim"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Serve the log, drive nothing. Two tabs attaching at once: one wins the lease and drives, the other tails."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The drive throws"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Logged server-side. It cannot be reported to a response that is already streaming; the run's own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"},{"type":"text","value":" event is that channel."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Serverless platforms need a keep-alive for the background drive. Pass\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"waitUntil: (promise) => ctx.waitUntil(promise)"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"client-reconnect-and-continue","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client: reconnect and continue"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A mid-stream drop needs nothing: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" reconnects with the last offset and\nthe server replays from the log."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const chat = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <button onClick={() => void chat.sendMessage('Refactor the auth module')}>\n      Send ({chat.messages.length} messages)\n    </button>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A full reload is different. The page comes back with no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Last-Event-ID"},{"type":"text","value":", so it\nhas to ask which run is still going and then join it from the start with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"joinRun"},{"type":"text","value":" (a read-only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"offset=-1"},{"type":"text","value":"), which is exactly the handler\nabove. That "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" is what claims the run, so joining and taking over are the same\nrequest."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\nimport { useEffect, useState } from 'react'\nimport type { StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nexport function ResumeInFlight({ threadId }: { threadId: string }) {\n  const [chunks, setChunks] = useState<Array<StreamChunk>>([])\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const controller = new AbortController()\n    const connection = fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat')\n\n    async function rejoin(): Promise<void> {\n      const response = await fetch(\n        `/api/chat/active?threadId=${encodeURIComponent(threadId)}`,\n        { signal: controller.signal },\n      )\n      const body: unknown = await response.json()\n      if (typeof body !== 'object' || body === null || !('runId' in body)) return\n      const runId = body.runId\n      if (typeof runId !== 'string') return\n      for await (const chunk of connection.joinRun(runId, controller.signal)) {\n        setChunks((previous) => [...previous, chunk])\n      }\n    }\n\n    void rejoin().catch(() => {\n      // The run finished, or there was none. Nothing to rejoin.\n    })\n    return () => controller.abort()\n  }, [threadId])\n\n  return <p>{chunks.length} events replayed</p>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The \"which run\" endpoint is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore.findActiveRun"},{"type":"text","value":", which resolves a live run\nfrom the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"stable"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" rather than the ephemeral run id a single turn may\nmint several of. It is an optional store method, so feature-detect it."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { memoryPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\nconst { runs } = persistence.stores\n\nexport async function GET(request: Request) {\n  const threadId = new URL(request.url).searchParams.get('threadId')\n  if (threadId === null) {\n    return new Response('threadId is required', { status: 400 })\n  }\n  // Optional on the RunStore contract, so a backend that omits it degrades to\n  // \"no active run\" instead of throwing.\n  const active = await runs.findActiveRun(threadId)\n  return Response.json({ runId: active?.runId ?? null })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All four HTTP connection adapters ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchServerSentEvents"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchHttpStream"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xhrServerSentEvents"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xhrHttpStream"},{"type":"text","value":") expose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"joinRun"},{"type":"text","value":". For NDJSON, pair it\nwith "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumeHttpResponse"},{"type":"text","value":" on the server; the driver wiring is identical."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"detach-vs-cancel","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Detach vs cancel"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is the part that is easy to get wrong, and getting it wrong is\nexpensive."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A user pressing Stop and a user closing the tab produce the\n"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"identical"}]},{"type":"text","value":" connection close. There is nothing in the disconnect to tell them\napart."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"So intent is never inferred from the disconnect. It arrives "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"out of band"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and\nthere are exactly two bands, and either one is authoritative:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable"}]},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"requestRunCancel(runs, runId)"},{"type":"text","value":" records "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancelRequested"},{"type":"text","value":" on\nthe run record. This is the only channel that reaches a run being driven by a\n"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"different"}]},{"type":"text","value":" host than the one the cancel request landed on, which is the\nnormal case for a detached run."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In-process"}]},{"type":"text","value":": abort the run's own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortController"},{"type":"text","value":" with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_CANCEL_REASON"},{"type":"text","value":". Core reads that reason back when it builds "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortInfo"},{"type":"text","value":",\nso "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortInfo.cancelRequested"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" for that abort and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" for a\nplain disconnect. Fast path when the cancel reaches the driving host."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A cancel endpoint should do "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"both"}]},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"requestRunCancel"},{"type":"text","value":" deliberately writes no\nstatus: recording intent is not the same as the run having stopped, and only the\ndriver knows when the agent is actually dead and the sandbox is gone."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { RUN_CANCEL_REASON, requestRunCancel } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { memoryPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\nconst { runs } = persistence.stores\n\n/**\n * Runs this process is currently driving. Only ever a fast path: a run driven by\n * another replica is absent here, and the durable band is what reaches it.\n */\nconst driving = new Map<string, AbortController>()\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const body: unknown = await request.json()\n  if (typeof body !== 'object' || body === null || !('threadId' in body)) {\n    return new Response('threadId is required', { status: 400 })\n  }\n  const threadId = body.threadId\n  if (typeof threadId !== 'string') {\n    return new Response('threadId must be a string', { status: 400 })\n  }\n\n  const active = await runs.findActiveRun(threadId)\n  if (!active) return new Response(null, { status: 204 })\n\n  // Band 1: durable, so a remote driver observes it on its next teardown.\n  await requestRunCancel(runs, active.runId)\n  // Band 2: in-process, so a co-located driver stops immediately.\n  driving.get(active.runId)?.abort(RUN_CANCEL_REASON)\n\n  return new Response(null, { status: 204 })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Populate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"driving"},{"type":"text","value":" where you create the run's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortController"},{"type":"text","value":": in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"POST"},{"type":"text","value":"\nhandler for a fresh run, and in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"controllerFor"},{"type":"text","value":" on the takeover path."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On the client, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat.stop()"},{"type":"text","value":" sends the server nothing. A durable run then\ncontinues, because a refresh looks the same. Call the endpoint too."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function StoppableChat({ threadId }: { threadId: string }) {\n  const chat = useChat({\n    threadId,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  })\n\n  async function stopRun(): Promise<void> {\n    // Local: stop rendering the stream immediately.\n    chat.stop()\n    // Remote: tell the server this was intent, not a lost connection.\n    await fetch('/api/chat/cancel', {\n      method: 'POST',\n      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n      body: JSON.stringify({ threadId }),\n    })\n  }\n\n  return <button onClick={() => void stopRun()}>Stop</button>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"what-each-path-writes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What each path writes"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Event"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Delivery log"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disconnect, durable, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachOnDisconnect"},{"type":"text","value":" on, no cancel recorded"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keeps the sandbox; writes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedSince"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxKey"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Writes "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"nothing"}]},{"type":"text","value":"; the record stays "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'running'"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Left "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"open"}]},{"type":"text","value":", no terminal appended"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cancel (either band)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Destroys the sandbox, always, regardless of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"destroyOnComplete"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Writes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'aborted'"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finishedAt"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"},{"type":"text","value":", then closed"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disconnect on a non-durable run"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Destroys the sandbox"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Writes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'aborted'"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"},{"type":"text","value":", then closed"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The delivery-log column is what makes takeover work at all. A detached run's log\nhas to stay open and terminal-free: a closed log ends the attaching client's\nreplay at the prefix, and a stored synthetic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"},{"type":"text","value":" is a chunk the takeover's\njournal replay cannot reproduce, so alignment diverges and a perfectly healthy run\nis recorded as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'failed'"},{"type":"text","value":". See "},{"type":"link","href":"#rundetachedcapability","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunDetachedCapability"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor how the verdict reaches the transport."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keepAlive"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"destroyOnComplete: false"},{"type":"text","value":" govern "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"successful completion"}]},{"type":"text","value":" only.\nThey never keep a sandbox alive through a cancel."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Get this backwards in either direction and it hurts: a Stop button that only\ncalls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat.stop()"},{"type":"text","value":" leaks running sandboxes that keep spending tokens with nobody\nwatching, and treating every disconnect as a cancel kills a user's ten-minute\nrefactor on a wifi blip."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"what-cancel-means-on-a-provider-that-cannot-kill","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What cancel means on a provider that cannot kill"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On a provider whose capabilities declare "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"killableProcesses: false"},{"type":"text","value":" (Daytona,\nVercel, and Cloudflare among the bundled ones (see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./providers#killableprocesses-across-the-bundled-providers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the measured table"}]},{"type":"text","value":")\nand no signal reaches the agent process: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kill()"},{"type":"text","value":" does not stop it, and no\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortSignal"},{"type":"text","value":" crosses the provider boundary. There, "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the sandbox destroy the\ncancel path performs is not cleanup, it is the cancel"}]},{"type":"text","value":". It is the only mechanism\nthat actually stops the agent. Wire a cancel to anything less (mark the record\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'aborted'"},{"type":"text","value":", close the log, skip the destroy) and what you have built is\n\"marked cancelled, still running\": the UI says stopped while the agent keeps\nworking and the sandbox keeps billing."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"single-writer-safety","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Single-writer safety"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Only one host may write a run, and the client has no safety\nnet below its offset de-dup. If two hosts both snapshot the log, both compute a\n\"remainder\", and both append it, the same logical chunk lands twice under two\ndifferent offsets, so it looks new to the client, and the stream processor\napplies text and tool-argument deltas unconditionally. You get doubled prose and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{\"a\":1}{\"a\":1}"},{"type":"text","value":" tool arguments. Takeover is by definition two hosts wanting one\nrun, so the exclusion has to be real."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxRunDriver"},{"type":"text","value":" wires it for you, in three layers:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A lease."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The entire drive runs inside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"LockStore.withLock"},{"type":"text","value":" on a per-run\nkey, so the snapshot and every append after it are one critical section. A\nlease-backed lock aborts the drive's signal the moment ownership is lost."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An epoch."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each successful claim bumps "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunRecord.driverEpoch"},{"type":"text","value":". The log\nre-reads it periodically and refuses to append once a higher epoch exists.\nThis covers what a lease cannot: a lock whose renewal is coarser than the\nrun's append rate, or one whose signal never fires at all."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quiescence."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The successor waits for the stored log to stop growing before\nits first append, so a predecessor that is still writing is "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"observed"}]},{"type":"text","value":" rather\nthan raced. The window defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DEFAULT_FENCE_QUIET_MS"},{"type":"text","value":" (5 seconds) and is\nconfigurable with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fenceQuietMs"},{"type":"text","value":". If the log never quiesces, the drive fails\ninstead of appending into a log another host is still writing."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"both-authoritative-channels-are-fenced","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both authoritative channels are fenced"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A run's facts live in "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"two"}]},{"type":"text","value":" places: its event log and its record. Fencing only\nthe log does not remove the harm, it relocates it. A superseded driver whose\nappend is refused folds that refusal into a terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs.update"},{"type":"text","value":", and the\nrecord then reads "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'failed'"},{"type":"text","value":" for a run the successor is healthily streaming.\nEvery consumer that branches on terminal status ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isTerminalRunStatus"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"findActiveRun"},{"type":"text","value":", a status poller, a reaper) would then believe a live run had\ndied, on the authority of a host that no longer owns it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"So both seams are closed, over the same claim:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The log."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A superseded driver's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"append"},{"type":"text","value":" is refused. The "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"first"}]},{"type":"text","value":" refusal\nlatches the fence permanently shut: every later append refuses immediately,\nwithout a re-read. That permanence matters, because the refused append is followed\ndirectly by the recovery path's own terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"},{"type":"text","value":", and that log belongs\nto the "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"successor"}]},{"type":"text","value":", so a dead host's error chunk would fail the stream for\nevery client attached to the healthy run."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The record."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A terminal-status "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":" from a lost claim is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"suppressed"}]},{"type":"text","value":":\nit resolves without writing. Non-terminal writes still pass through, including\na stale "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedSince"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxKey"},{"type":"text","value":". Those cannot make a live run look\nfinished, and the successor owns and overwrites them anyway."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The practical upshot for you: "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a terminal status on the record is trustworthy."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nA status poller may believe it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close()"},{"type":"text","value":" is outside both fences, deliberately. It runs on every teardown path\nincluding the teardown "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"caused"}]},{"type":"text","value":" by losing the claim, and a fenced "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close"},{"type":"text","value":" would\nwedge the record at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'running'"},{"type":"text","value":" with every live tailer parked forever: a\ndurability "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"read"},{"type":"text","value":" only ends when the log closes. A wedged run with parked clients\nis worse than the write being prevented."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"errors-you-can-branch-on","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Errors you can branch on"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  RunClaimLostError,\n  RunClaimNotAcquiredError,\n  RunDriverPipeOutsideClaimError,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nfunction describeDriveFailure(error: unknown): string {\n  if (error instanceof RunClaimNotAcquiredError) {\n    // 'terminal' | 'unknown' | 'superseded': normal, not a bug.\n    return `not driving ${error.runId}: ${error.reason}`\n  }\n  if (error instanceof RunClaimLostError) {\n    return `superseded mid-drive at epoch ${error.heldEpoch}`\n  }\n  if (error instanceof RunDriverPipeOutsideClaimError) {\n    // A programming error: the options object was taken apart and `pipe` called\n    // outside `claim`, so there is no epoch to fence with.\n    return `run ${error.runId}: pipe ran outside its claim`\n  }\n  throw error\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first two are ordinary outcomes of a contended takeover and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumeServerSentEventsResponse"},{"type":"text","value":" already swallows both. You will see them in\nlogs, not in responses."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"what-this-is-not","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What this is not"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is not airtight fencing. A predecessor paused (GC, VM suspend) for longer than\nthe quiescence window, between its last fence check and its append landing at the\nbackend, can still write one batch. Closing that needs a compare-and-set on the\ndurability write, which "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamDurability.append"},{"type":"text","value":" does not offer. The mitigation\nis deployment-level: use a lease-backed distributed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"LockStore"},{"type":"text","value":", and keep\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fenceQuietMs"},{"type":"text","value":" above the lease's renewal interval."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"replay-and-divergence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Replay and divergence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A takeover does not resume the journal from where the dead host stopped. It\nre-reads the journal "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"from byte zero"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and re-translates it, which reproduces\nchunks the client already has. Alignment is what makes that safe: the stored log\nis read once, the replay is verified against it by fingerprint, the matching\nprefix is suppressed, and only the remainder is appended and delivered. The log\n"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"is"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the checkpoint, so there is no window in which a checkpoint and the log can\ndisagree. The precedence rule this implements: "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the log wins for what clients\nsee; the journal wins for where the taking-over driver resumes."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two properties make the comparison possible, and both are already true on the\njournaled path:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ids are deterministic."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A journaled run mints message ids from a run-scoped\ncounter ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<runId>-0"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<runId>-1"},{"type":"text","value":", …) rather than a timestamp plus randomness."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Alignment runs only on an attach."}]},{"type":"text","value":" On a fresh run the transform's premise\n(\"this stream is a replay of what is already stored\") is false, and aligning\nanyway would match a fresh run's own chunks against pre-existing log entries\nand silently "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"suppress"}]},{"type":"text","value":" them. That is data loss, not a slow path."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"journalreplaydivergederror","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"JournalReplayDivergedError"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the replay produces a different chunk than the log already holds at that\nindex, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"JournalReplayDivergedError"},{"type":"text","value":" is thrown with the index and both\nfingerprints."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { JournalReplayDivergedError } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nfunction report(error: unknown): string {\n  if (error instanceof JournalReplayDivergedError) {\n    return `diverged at ${error.index}: stored ${error.stored}, replayed ${error.replayed}`\n  }\n  throw error\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read it plainly: "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the agent's replay produced a different sequence of events\nthan the log already delivered."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Translation stopped being deterministic. The\nrealistic causes are an id generator that is not run-scoped, a translator that\nconsults the clock, or a journal that was rewritten (most often a reused\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What to do about it: treat it as a bug to fix, not a condition to recover from.\nDo not catch it and continue. The log is authoritative and already went to the\nclient, so forwarding past a mismatch delivers a stream whose prefix and suffix\ndisagree about message identity, and the client cannot survive that. Log the\nindex and both fingerprints, let the run fail, and check "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":" uniqueness\nfirst."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One tolerance exists. On adapters that splice host-tool-bridge events into their\noutput ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-codex"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-claude-code"},{"type":"text","value":"), the log holds "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CUSTOM"},{"type":"text","value":"\nchunks fired by "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"live"}]},{"type":"text","value":" tool execution, which a replay runs no tools to reproduce.\nAlignment skips those as out-of-band, up to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DEFAULT_MAX_OUT_OF_BAND_SKIP"},{"type":"text","value":" (64)\nconsecutive entries. The bound is what keeps this a tolerance rather than a\nsearch: unbounded, a genuine determinism regression would scan forward looking\nfor any fingerprint that happens to match."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"configuration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All of these live under "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox(sandbox, { durability: { … } })"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What it does"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"adapter"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"required"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The run's delivery-durable event log. Same instance you hand the transport."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"journal"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/tmp/tanstack-runs"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DEFAULT_JOURNAL_DIR"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Journal directory inside the sandbox."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachOnDisconnect"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" whenever durability is wired"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whether a disconnect detaches instead of destroying the sandbox."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"attach"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read an existing run's journal instead of starting an agent. Set by an attach route's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"drive"},{"type":"text","value":", never by a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"POST"},{"type":"text","value":" handler."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pollIntervalMs"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"adapter default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Journal poll interval for providers that cannot follow a file."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There is deliberately no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedRunTtl"},{"type":"text","value":" here. The only actor that enforces a\nTTL is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reapDetachedRuns"},{"type":"text","value":", which runs from a cron with no chat request in\nflight, so it cannot read anything "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" publishes on the\nper-request capability bus, so a TTL stored here could only ever go unread,\nwhile the sweep took its own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedRunTtlMs"},{"type":"text","value":", and the two would silently\ndisagree. So the sweep's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedRunTtlMs"},{"type":"text","value":" (milliseconds, no default, no\nstring parsing) is the single source of truth; see "},{"type":"link","href":"./reaping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reaping &\nRetention"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for sizing it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The reaper ships, but nothing schedules it."}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reapDetachedRuns"},{"type":"text","value":" from\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" is the sweep. It reads the optional\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore.listReclaimable({ now, ttlMs })"},{"type":"text","value":", which returns runs that are\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'running'"},{"type":"text","value":", have a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedSince"},{"type":"text","value":", and whose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedSince <= now - ttlMs"},{"type":"text","value":"\n(inclusive). It drives each run its out-of-band journal probe says already finished\nto a terminal status so the transcript lands, cancels and terminalizes the ones\npast "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedRunTtlMs"},{"type":"text","value":", and destroys the sandbox named by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxKey"},{"type":"text","value":" through\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxReclaimer"},{"type":"text","value":". It never clears "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedSince"},{"type":"text","value":": that marker is the evidence\nits TTL accounting selected the run on. (The takeover path clears it, because a\nviewer is attached again.)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is a plain async function with no timer and no daemon, so "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"calling it on a\nschedule is your job"}]},{"type":"text","value":": a cron route, a queue consumer, a Durable Object\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alarm()"},{"type":"text","value":", a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"waitUntil"},{"type":"text","value":". Treat that as a hard requirement of wiring\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"},{"type":"text","value":", not a nice-to-have: until something invokes it, nothing closes a\ndetached run's delivery log, so every attached client parks forever,\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedRunTtlMs"},{"type":"text","value":" is enforced by nothing, and the sandbox bills indefinitely."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./reaping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reaping & Retention"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is the whole picture: the sweep's outcomes, why\nit never drives a run to find out whether it finished, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pruneJournals"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reclaimSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":", ready-to-paste schedules for Node, Vercel Cron, and a\nCloudflare "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alarm()"},{"type":"text","value":", and how to size the TTL against the sweep interval."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachOnDisconnect: false"},{"type":"text","value":" to keep today's destroy-on-disconnect cost\nprofile while still getting resumable "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"delivery"}]},{"type":"text","value":": a reload replays the log, but\nthe agent does not survive the disconnect. An explicit cancel destroys the\nsandbox either way."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"detachableruncapability","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DetachableRunCapability"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A neutral boolean that core owns. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" provides it as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" only when a\nrun is genuinely durable; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" reads it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getOptional"},{"type":"text","value":" to decide\nwhether an abort is terminal ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'aborted'"},{"type":"text","value":") or a detach (write nothing). It lives\nin core so the two packages can agree without either importing the other. A\npersistence → sandbox import would invert the layering. Read it in your own\nmiddleware if you need the same distinction; absent means \"not detachable\", which\nis every app that has not wired durability."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"rundetachedcapability","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunDetachedCapability"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Its past-tense counterpart, also owned by core. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DetachableRunCapability"},{"type":"text","value":" is\npublished at setup and only says a disconnect "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"may"}]},{"type":"text","value":" be survived;\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunDetachedCapability"},{"type":"text","value":" is published on the abort path by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":"'s detach\nbranch and says the run "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"was"}]},{"type":"text","value":" detached. The agent is still working and a later\nattach can take it over."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Its consumer is the durable delivery sink behind "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toServerSentEventsResponse"},{"type":"text","value":" /\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toHttpResponse"},{"type":"text","value":". Without the verdict the sink terminalizes every abort, which\ndefeats takeover (see the table above). The fact travels on the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"stream object\nitself"}]},{"type":"text","value":", so there is nothing to wire: passing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":"'s stream to the response\nhelper is already mandatory, and both sides hold the same object."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { provideRunDetached } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { CapabilityContext } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// You do not write this. `withSandbox` does, on its detach branch, from a hook\n// that already holds the middleware context. Shown only so the fact is legible.\nfunction markRunDetached(ctx: CapabilityContext): void {\n  provideRunDetached(ctx, true)\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Only a plain, intentless disconnect of a detachable run publishes it. An explicit\ncancel in either band, a disconnect on a non-detachable run,\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachOnDisconnect: false"},{"type":"text","value":", a provider failure, and a normal finish all leave it\nunpublished, and the sink appends its terminal and closes the log exactly as it\nalways has. Core additionally refuses to treat an abort carrying\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_CANCEL_REASON"},{"type":"text","value":" as a detach, whatever a middleware publishes. A user pressing\nStop always gets a closed, terminal log."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"requirements","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirements"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A real "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"LockStore"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InMemoryLockStore"},{"type":"text","value":" cannot coordinate across hosts: it\nserializes claims within one process, and the signal it hands out is a fresh\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AbortController().signal"},{"type":"text","value":" that is never aborted, so the lease can never report a\nloss. Two replicas can then drive one run and duplicate its log. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":"\nemits a warning when durability is wired over an in-memory lock (including when\nno lock is wired at all, since the fallback "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"is"}]},{"type":"text","value":" in-memory). See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../advanced/locks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Locks"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A caller-supplied "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A durable run throws\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DurableRunIdRequiredError"},{"type":"text","value":" at the start of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chatStream"},{"type":"text","value":" when none is passed.\nThat is deliberately loud, because the alternative is invisible: an\nadapter-generated id produces a journal path no successor host can recompute, so\nthe run streams normally, records normally, and is silently unrecoverable. You\nwould only discover it during an incident."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The run record's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":", on an attaching run."}]},{"type":"text","value":" An ATTACHING durable run\nthrows "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DurableThreadIdRequiredError"},{"type":"text","value":" when driven without the run record's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" rides in "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"every"}]},{"type":"text","value":" emitted chunk, so an attach that\ngenerates a fresh one replays a stream whose very first chunk already differs\nfrom the stored log. Alignment fails at index 0 with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"JournalReplayThreadIdMismatchError"},{"type":"text","value":", a subclass of\n"},{"type":"link","href":"#journalreplaydivergederror","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"JournalReplayDivergedError"}]},{"type":"text","value":" reported above,\neven though the agent behaved identically. This is the asymmetry to keep\nstraight: a durable "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fresh"}]},{"type":"text","value":" run needs no caller "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":", because that run is\nwhat establishes it, but a durable run that is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"attaching"}]},{"type":"text","value":" must reuse the\none already on the record, which is exactly the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId: input.threadId"},{"type":"text","value":"\nforwarded by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"driveRun"},{"type":"text","value":" above."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":" that persists every durable-run field."}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createOrResume"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"get"},{"type":"text","value":" are required; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":" must accept and round-trip all of\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finishedAt"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"usage"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxKey"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedSince"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancelRequested"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"driverEpoch"},{"type":"text","value":". The last four are exactly what a\nhand-written backend tends to omit, and each one breaks a specific\nmechanism: no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"driverEpoch"},{"type":"text","value":" means no fencing, no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancelRequested"},{"type":"text","value":" means Stop\ncannot reach a remote driver, no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedSince"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxKey"},{"type":"text","value":" means nothing can\nreclaim the sandbox. Two invariants also hold: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createOrResume"},{"type":"text","value":" returns an\nexisting record "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"unchanged"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (that is what makes resuming safe), and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":" on\nan unknown "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":" is a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"no-op"}]},{"type":"text","value":" that must not throw."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"findActiveRun"},{"type":"text","value":" is required: it is how you rejoin by thread. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listReclaimable"},{"type":"text","value":"\nis optional and feature-detected, but "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reapDetachedRuns"},{"type":"text","value":" needs it to have\nanything to sweep: a store without it cannot be reaped at all."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"adapter-authors","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adapter authors"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you are writing a harness adapter rather than an application, three exports\nare yours: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getSandboxDurability"},{"type":"text","value":" reads the resolved durability off the\ncapability bus, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"journalOptionsFor"},{"type":"text","value":" turns it into the journal option\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spawnNdjson"},{"type":"text","value":" takes, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alignedIfAttaching"},{"type":"text","value":" applies alignment on attach only.\nWrap the "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"merged"}]},{"type":"text","value":" output stream, never the pre-merge translator, or you compare\nagainst a stream the log never contained. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./harnesses","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Harnesses"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"see-also","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See also"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./durable-runs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable Runs Explained"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the mental model behind this page, in\nplain language and with no code. Start there if any of the above felt abrupt"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./journal","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Run Journal"}]},{"type":"text","value":": where the agent's output lives, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":"\nuniqueness, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alignToStoredLog"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./durability","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sandbox Instance Durability"}]},{"type":"text","value":": keeping the sandbox itself\nfindable across processes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./lifecycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lifecycle & Snapshots"}]},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"destroyOnComplete"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keepAlive"},{"type":"text","value":", and\nsnapshots"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../resumable-streams/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resumable Streams"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../resumable-streams/advanced","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Advanced"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the delivery log, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"joinRun"},{"type":"text","value":", and\noffset ownership"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../advanced/locks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Locks"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the distributed lock a durable run requires"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../persistence/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistence overview"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../persistence/store-reference","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Store Reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the shared\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"durability-is-one-opt-in-not-two","text":"Durability is one opt-in, not two","level":2},{"id":"both-routes-must-address-the-same-backend-stream","text":"Both routes must address the same backend stream","level":2},{"id":"server-start-a-durable-run","text":"Server: start a durable run","level":2},{"id":"on-memorystream-raise-the-first-chunk-deadline","text":"On memoryStream, raise the first-chunk deadline","level":3},{"id":"server-take-the-run-over","text":"Server: take the run over","level":2},{"id":"client-reconnect-and-continue","text":"Client: reconnect and continue","level":2},{"id":"detach-vs-cancel","text":"Detach vs cancel","level":2},{"id":"what-each-path-writes","text":"What each path writes","level":3},{"id":"what-cancel-means-on-a-provider-that-cannot-kill","text":"What cancel means on a provider that cannot kill","level":3},{"id":"single-writer-safety","text":"Single-writer safety","level":2},{"id":"both-authoritative-channels-are-fenced","text":"Both authoritative channels are fenced","level":3},{"id":"errors-you-can-branch-on","text":"Errors you can branch on","level":3},{"id":"what-this-is-not","text":"What this is not","level":3},{"id":"replay-and-divergence","text":"Replay and divergence","level":2},{"id":"journalreplaydivergederror","text":"JournalReplayDivergedError","level":3},{"id":"configuration","text":"Configuration","level":2},{"id":"detachableruncapability","text":"DetachableRunCapability","level":3},{"id":"rundetachedcapability","text":"RunDetachedCapability","level":3},{"id":"requirements","text":"Requirements","level":2},{"id":"adapter-authors","text":"Adapter authors","level":2},{"id":"see-also","text":"See also","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nA sandboxed coding agent can work for ten minutes. A browser tab does not last\nten minutes. Users refresh, close the laptop, lose wifi, and hit a load balancer\nthat lands the next request on a different replica.\n\nWithout durability wired, the disconnect is fatal: `withSandbox`'s abort path\ndestroys the sandbox on every abort, deliberately, because closing the agent's\nIO stream does **not** kill the agent process (a Docker `exec` survives its\nclient), so destroying the container is the only reliable way to stop it burning\ntokens. Correct for a cancel. Ruinous for a refresh.\n\nDurable runs split those two cases apart. A disconnect **detaches**: the agent\nkeeps working, the sandbox stays up, and the run record remembers that nobody is\nwatching. A later request **takes the run over**, replays what was already\ndelivered, and continues streaming the rest.\n\nThis page is the wiring. It builds on two pages you should read first:\n[The Run Journal](./journal) (where the agent's output lives, and why it is a\nfile rather than a pipe) and\n[Resumable Streams](../resumable-streams/overview) (the delivery log a client\nreconnects to).\n\n## Durability is one opt-in, not two\n\n`withSandbox` takes `runs` and `durability`. A run is durable only when **both**\nare present, because either alone is useless: a record with no event log cannot\nbe replayed, and a log with no record cannot be found, claimed, or reaped. There\nis no half-configured state. Pass one and you get exactly today's behavior,\nsilently and with no warning, because you have not asked for durability.\n\nPass the **same** `RunStore` chat persistence uses, so one record describes the\nrun instead of two that disagree.\n\n## Both routes must address the same backend stream\n\nRead this before the snippets. A `StreamDurability` is bound to **one run**,\nand `durableStream(request, options)` resolves which run through core's\n`resolveResumeRunId`: the `X-Run-Id` header first, then `?runId` on the\nrequest URL. Core's own `memoryStream` uses the same resolver, so no two\ndurability adapters can disagree about which run a request names.\n\n- A **POST from `@tanstack/ai-client`** carries the run id in the `X-Run-Id`\n  header, not the URL. The POST URL stays byte-identical to a plain, non-durable\n  chat request.\n- A **GET join** carries it in the URL instead: `joinRun` requests\n  `?offset=-1&runId=<runId>`.\n\nSo `durableStream(request, durableOptions)` on both routes resolves the same\n`agent-runs/<runId>` stream, whichever way the run id arrived. Nothing to rewrite,\nnothing to force onto a copy of the URL:\n\n```ts\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\n\n// The external Durable Streams backend every replica can reach. See\n// ../resumable-streams/advanced for the full option set (auth headers, batch\n// size, reconnect tuning).\nexport const durableOptions = {\n  server: 'https://streams.example.com',\n  streamPrefix: 'agent-runs',\n}\n```\n\nA request naming no run at all (neither header nor query) throws rather than\nsilently producing into a stream no attach request could ever name:\n\n```\ndurableStream: a runId is required: send it as an X-Run-Id header or a\n?runId query param\n```\n\nThat only bites a client that bypasses `@tanstack/ai-client` (a custom\n`fetch`, a hand-rolled reconnect): make sure it sends one of the two, an\n`X-Run-Id` header on the POST or `?runId` on the URL. A mid-stream SSE\nreconnect to the POST route still works. It arrives carrying `Last-Event-ID` and the\nsame `X-Run-Id` header the original POST used, so `durableStream` resolves the run and\nhonors the resume offset.\n\nAnd a caller with no live `Request` at all (a cron, a Durable Object `alarm()`)\nsynthesizes one from scratch instead. See the reaper's own\n`durabilityFor` in [Reaping & Retention](./reaping).\n\n## Server: start a durable run\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { withLocks } from '@tanstack/ai/locks'\nimport { claudeCodeText } from '@tanstack/ai-claude-code'\nimport { memoryPersistence, withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\n// The options from the section above.\nimport { durableOptions } from './durability'\n// Your distributed LockStore. `InMemoryLockStore` is NOT enough here. See\n// \"Requirements\" below.\nimport { locks } from './locks'\n// Your `defineSandbox(...)` result.\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\n\n// Development stand-in. A durable run needs a store every replica can read;\n// see ../persistence/build-your-own-adapter.\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\nconst { runs } = persistence.stores\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  // ONE adapter instance, handed to both the middleware and the transport, so\n  // the journal path and the delivery log describe the same run.\n  // `@tanstack/ai-client` sends `X-Run-Id` on every POST, which is exactly what\n  // `durableStream` resolves first, so this already addresses\n  // `agent-runs/<runId>`: the same stream the attach route below reads.\n  const adapter = durableStream(request, durableOptions)\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: claudeCodeText('claude-opus-4-8'),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    // Required for a durable run. Omit it and `chatStream` throws\n    // `DurableRunIdRequiredError`. See \"Requirements\".\n    runId,\n    middleware: [\n      withPersistence(persistence),\n      withLocks(locks),\n      withSandbox(sandbox, {\n        runs,\n        durability: { adapter },\n      }),\n    ],\n  })\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, { durability: { adapter } })\n}\n```\n\nThree things changed compared with a plain sandboxed chat endpoint, and each one\nis load bearing:\n\n- **`runs` + `durability`** turn on detach-on-disconnect and publish\n  `DetachableRunCapability` on the bus, which is how `withPersistence` learns\n  that an abort on this run is a detach rather than a cancel.\n- **`runId` is forwarded**, not generated. The journal path, the deterministic\n  message-id generator, **and the backend stream name** are all derived from it,\n  so a successor host can only resume a run whose `runId` it can recompute.\n- **The adapter instance is shared** between `withSandbox` and the response, and it is\n  keyed by that same `runId`. That is what makes the attach route below address this\n  very stream.\n\nOne thing must **not** be there: an `abortController` that mirrors `request.signal`.\n\nA plain sandboxed endpoint mirrors it, because there a disconnect should end the run.\nOn a durable run it destroys the thing you are trying to protect. Aborting the run\nmakes `chat()` return at its cancellation check right after middleware `setup`, so the\nharness adapter's `chatStream` is never called and **the agent inside the sandbox you\njust spent minutes building is never launched**. Switch tabs while the UI still says\n\"starting the sandbox\" and you come back to an empty log for a run that did nothing.\nNo takeover can recover it either, because an agent that never started wrote no\njournal to replay.\n\nThe disconnect still reaches `withSandbox` without it. The durable transport notifies\nthe run the moment the response body is cancelled, **without** aborting it, and then:\n\n- `withSandbox` stamps `detachedSince` and `sandboxKey`, and publishes the detach\n  verdict.\n- The run keeps draining into its still-open delivery log.\n- A rejoining client tails that log.\n\nPassing `runs` plus `durability` buys all of that. There is nothing else to wire.\n\nA genuine stop is unaffected, because it arrives out of band (see\n[Detach vs cancel](#detach-vs-cancel)). That is the only channel that can tell \"the\nuser wants this stopped\" from \"the user closed a tab\", since both are the same socket\nclose on the wire.\n\n### On `memoryStream`, raise the first-chunk deadline\n\nA rejoin during the sandbox build fails with\n`Memory stream run produced no data within 100ms`, and the run it calls gone is\nhealthy. `memoryStream` gives up on a from-start rejoin when the run produces no chunk\nwithin `firstChunkDeadlineMs`, which defaults to 100ms. That default fits chat, where\nan in-flight run's log already holds chunks. A sandboxed run emits nothing until\n`ensure` has built the sandbox and cloned the repo.\n\nRaise it on **every** handle for the run. The `GET` that serves the rejoin is the call\nthat actually applies it:\n\n```ts\nimport { memoryStream } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\n// Longer than your slowest `ensure`.\nconst FIRST_CHUNK_DEADLINE_MS = 15 * 60_000;\n\n// POST (the producer) and GET (the rejoin) alike.\nexport function adapterFor(request: Request) {\n  return memoryStream(request, {\n    firstChunkDeadlineMs: FIRST_CHUNK_DEADLINE_MS,\n  });\n}\n```\n\nFailing fast buys nothing once you gate the rejoin on `findActiveRun`, which already\nexcludes a run that is really gone before the join is attempted.\n\nTwo things make this a backstop rather than the main defence:\n\n- `durableStream` runs no first-chunk deadline at all. Its `read` parks for a live\n  reader, so an empty in-flight log simply waits.\n- A fresh durable producer appends one `CUSTOM` `run.accepted` chunk\n  (`RUN_ACCEPTED_EVENT`) before it pulls the producer stream, so a rejoin attaches in\n  milliseconds instead of waiting on the harness. That chunk also keeps the client from\n  abandoning the join, since `ai-client` gives up on a rejoin that receives nothing for\n  2s and a sandbox build always takes longer.\n\n## Server: take the run over\n\nThe takeover happens in the `GET` handler that already serves resumes. Add a\n`driver` and the same request that replays the log also claims the run and keeps\ndriving it.\n\nEvery adapter here comes from the **same** `durableStream(request, durableOptions)`\nthe `POST` route used, so the log this route replays, the log the drive appends\nto, and the log the producing route wrote are provably one stream:\n`agent-runs/<runId>`.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, resumeServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { withLocks } from '@tanstack/ai/locks'\nimport { claudeCodeText } from '@tanstack/ai-claude-code'\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\nimport { memoryPersistence, withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { sandboxRunDriver, withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n// The same backend options as the POST route.\nimport { durableOptions } from './durability'\nimport { locks } from './locks'\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\nimport type { StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\nconst { messages: messageStore, runs } = persistence.stores\n\n/**\n * The claim hands `drive` an `AbortSignal` that fires the moment this host loses\n * ownership; `chat()` takes an `AbortController`. Mirror one onto the other so a\n * lost claim actually stops the drive.\n */\nfunction controllerFor(signal: AbortSignal): AbortController {\n  const controller = new AbortController()\n  const abort = (): void => controller.abort(signal.reason)\n  if (signal.aborted) abort()\n  else signal.addEventListener('abort', abort, { once: true })\n  return controller\n}\n\nexport function GET(request: Request) {\n  async function* driveRun(input: {\n    runId: string\n    threadId: string\n    signal: AbortSignal\n  }): AsyncIterable<StreamChunk> {\n    // The client sent no history: it is reconnecting, not asking a question.\n    const stored = await messageStore.loadThread(input.threadId)\n    const stream = chat({\n      adapter: claudeCodeText('claude-opus-4-8'),\n      messages: stored,\n      threadId: input.threadId,\n      runId: input.runId,\n      abortController: controllerFor(input.signal),\n      middleware: [\n        withPersistence(persistence),\n        withLocks(locks),\n        withSandbox(sandbox, {\n          runs,\n          // `attach: true` is the whole difference: the harness tails the run's\n          // EXISTING journal instead of starting a second agent. It belongs\n          // here and never on `chat()`, which has no sandbox vocabulary. It\n          // is set only by an attach route, never by a POST handler.\n          durability: {\n            // `request` already names this run (`?runId` on an attach GET),\n            // so `durableStream` resolves the same `agent-runs/<runId>` the\n            // journal replay aligns against.\n            adapter: durableStream(request, durableOptions),\n            attach: true,\n          },\n        }),\n      ],\n    })\n    for await (const chunk of stream) yield chunk\n  }\n\n  return resumeServerSentEventsResponse({\n    // The replaying adapter is the one adapter here whose `resumeFrom()` matters,\n    // and the offset it must return (`?offset=-1` from a join, or an SSE\n    // reconnect's `Last-Event-ID`) lives on the incoming request. `durableStream`\n    // resolves the run the same way on every route, `X-Run-Id` header first,\n    // then `?runId`, so this addresses the same `agent-runs/<runId>` the POST\n    // route wrote, whichever way a given request names the run.\n    adapter: durableStream(request, durableOptions),\n    driver: sandboxRunDriver({\n      request,\n      runs,\n      locks,\n      // Per-run log factory. Core resolves the id from this same request\n      // through the same `resolveResumeRunId` `durableStream` uses, so every\n      // call for this run talks to the same backend stream and `snapshot()`\n      // sees this host's own appends. The state lives in the Durable Streams\n      // backend, not this process.\n      durability: () => durableStream(request, durableOptions),\n      drive: driveRun,\n    }),\n  })\n}\n```\n\nThe response is **byte-identical** whether or not you pass `driver`: it still\nreplays from the durability log. The drive runs beside it, appending to the run's\nproducer-side log, and the response tails what lands. That separation is what\nlets a taken-over run keep `chat()`'s normal middleware path, so\n`withPersistence`'s `onFinish` still saves the transcript of a run that finished\nwhile detached.\n\nEverything about the takeover is **total by construction**. Every failure is\nlogged and swallowed, and the response still serves the log:\n\n| Situation | What happens |\n| --- | --- |\n| No run id on the request, or no record | Serve the log, drive nothing. |\n| The record is already terminal | Serve the log, drive nothing. A second tab attaching to a finished run must still see the transcript. |\n| Another host holds the claim | Serve the log, drive nothing. Two tabs attaching at once: one wins the lease and drives, the other tails. |\n| The drive throws | Logged server-side. It cannot be reported to a response that is already streaming; the run's own `RUN_ERROR` event is that channel. |\n\nServerless platforms need a keep-alive for the background drive. Pass\n`waitUntil: (promise) => ctx.waitUntil(promise)`.\n\n## Client: reconnect and continue\n\nA mid-stream drop needs nothing: `useChat` reconnects with the last offset and\nthe server replays from the log.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const chat = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <button onClick={() => void chat.sendMessage('Refactor the auth module')}>\n      Send ({chat.messages.length} messages)\n    </button>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nA full reload is different. The page comes back with no `Last-Event-ID`, so it\nhas to ask which run is still going and then join it from the start with\n`joinRun` (a read-only `GET` with `offset=-1`), which is exactly the handler\nabove. That `GET` is what claims the run, so joining and taking over are the same\nrequest.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\nimport { useEffect, useState } from 'react'\nimport type { StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nexport function ResumeInFlight({ threadId }: { threadId: string }) {\n  const [chunks, setChunks] = useState<Array<StreamChunk>>([])\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const controller = new AbortController()\n    const connection = fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat')\n\n    async function rejoin(): Promise<void> {\n      const response = await fetch(\n        `/api/chat/active?threadId=${encodeURIComponent(threadId)}`,\n        { signal: controller.signal },\n      )\n      const body: unknown = await response.json()\n      if (typeof body !== 'object' || body === null || !('runId' in body)) return\n      const runId = body.runId\n      if (typeof runId !== 'string') return\n      for await (const chunk of connection.joinRun(runId, controller.signal)) {\n        setChunks((previous) => [...previous, chunk])\n      }\n    }\n\n    void rejoin().catch(() => {\n      // The run finished, or there was none. Nothing to rejoin.\n    })\n    return () => controller.abort()\n  }, [threadId])\n\n  return <p>{chunks.length} events replayed</p>\n}\n```\n\nThe \"which run\" endpoint is `RunStore.findActiveRun`, which resolves a live run\nfrom the **stable** `threadId` rather than the ephemeral run id a single turn may\nmint several of. It is an optional store method, so feature-detect it.\n\n```ts\nimport { memoryPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\nconst { runs } = persistence.stores\n\nexport async function GET(request: Request) {\n  const threadId = new URL(request.url).searchParams.get('threadId')\n  if (threadId === null) {\n    return new Response('threadId is required', { status: 400 })\n  }\n  // Optional on the RunStore contract, so a backend that omits it degrades to\n  // \"no active run\" instead of throwing.\n  const active = await runs.findActiveRun(threadId)\n  return Response.json({ runId: active?.runId ?? null })\n}\n```\n\nAll four HTTP connection adapters (`fetchServerSentEvents`, `fetchHttpStream`,\n`xhrServerSentEvents`, `xhrHttpStream`) expose `joinRun`. For NDJSON, pair it\nwith `resumeHttpResponse` on the server; the driver wiring is identical.\n\n## Detach vs cancel\n\n**This is the part that is easy to get wrong, and getting it wrong is\nexpensive.** A user pressing Stop and a user closing the tab produce the\n*identical* connection close. There is nothing in the disconnect to tell them\napart.\n\nSo intent is never inferred from the disconnect. It arrives **out of band**, and\nthere are exactly two bands, and either one is authoritative:\n\n1. **Durable**: `requestRunCancel(runs, runId)` records `cancelRequested` on\n   the run record. This is the only channel that reaches a run being driven by a\n   *different* host than the one the cancel request landed on, which is the\n   normal case for a detached run.\n2. **In-process**: abort the run's own `AbortController` with\n   `RUN_CANCEL_REASON`. Core reads that reason back when it builds `AbortInfo`,\n   so `AbortInfo.cancelRequested` is `true` for that abort and `false` for a\n   plain disconnect. Fast path when the cancel reaches the driving host.\n\nA cancel endpoint should do **both**. `requestRunCancel` deliberately writes no\nstatus: recording intent is not the same as the run having stopped, and only the\ndriver knows when the agent is actually dead and the sandbox is gone.\n\n```ts\nimport { RUN_CANCEL_REASON, requestRunCancel } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { memoryPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\nconst { runs } = persistence.stores\n\n/**\n * Runs this process is currently driving. Only ever a fast path: a run driven by\n * another replica is absent here, and the durable band is what reaches it.\n */\nconst driving = new Map<string, AbortController>()\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const body: unknown = await request.json()\n  if (typeof body !== 'object' || body === null || !('threadId' in body)) {\n    return new Response('threadId is required', { status: 400 })\n  }\n  const threadId = body.threadId\n  if (typeof threadId !== 'string') {\n    return new Response('threadId must be a string', { status: 400 })\n  }\n\n  const active = await runs.findActiveRun(threadId)\n  if (!active) return new Response(null, { status: 204 })\n\n  // Band 1: durable, so a remote driver observes it on its next teardown.\n  await requestRunCancel(runs, active.runId)\n  // Band 2: in-process, so a co-located driver stops immediately.\n  driving.get(active.runId)?.abort(RUN_CANCEL_REASON)\n\n  return new Response(null, { status: 204 })\n}\n```\n\nPopulate `driving` where you create the run's `AbortController`: in the `POST`\nhandler for a fresh run, and in `controllerFor` on the takeover path.\n\nOn the client, `chat.stop()` sends the server nothing. A durable run then\ncontinues, because a refresh looks the same. Call the endpoint too.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function StoppableChat({ threadId }: { threadId: string }) {\n  const chat = useChat({\n    threadId,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  })\n\n  async function stopRun(): Promise<void> {\n    // Local: stop rendering the stream immediately.\n    chat.stop()\n    // Remote: tell the server this was intent, not a lost connection.\n    await fetch('/api/chat/cancel', {\n      method: 'POST',\n      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n      body: JSON.stringify({ threadId }),\n    })\n  }\n\n  return <button onClick={() => void stopRun()}>Stop</button>\n}\n```\n\n### What each path writes\n\n| Event | `withSandbox` | `withPersistence` | Delivery log |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Disconnect, durable, `detachOnDisconnect` on, no cancel recorded | Keeps the sandbox; writes `detachedSince` and `sandboxKey` | Writes **nothing**; the record stays `'running'` | Left **open**, no terminal appended |\n| Cancel (either band) | Destroys the sandbox, always, regardless of `destroyOnComplete` | Writes `'aborted'` with `finishedAt` | Terminal `RUN_ERROR`, then closed |\n| Disconnect on a non-durable run | Destroys the sandbox | Writes `'aborted'` | Terminal `RUN_ERROR`, then closed |\n\nThe delivery-log column is what makes takeover work at all. A detached run's log\nhas to stay open and terminal-free: a closed log ends the attaching client's\nreplay at the prefix, and a stored synthetic `RUN_ERROR` is a chunk the takeover's\njournal replay cannot reproduce, so alignment diverges and a perfectly healthy run\nis recorded as `'failed'`. See [`RunDetachedCapability`](#rundetachedcapability)\nfor how the verdict reaches the transport.\n\n`keepAlive` / `destroyOnComplete: false` govern *successful completion* only.\nThey never keep a sandbox alive through a cancel.\n\nGet this backwards in either direction and it hurts: a Stop button that only\ncalls `chat.stop()` leaks running sandboxes that keep spending tokens with nobody\nwatching, and treating every disconnect as a cancel kills a user's ten-minute\nrefactor on a wifi blip.\n\n### What cancel means on a provider that cannot kill\n\nOn a provider whose capabilities declare `killableProcesses: false` (Daytona,\nVercel, and Cloudflare among the bundled ones (see\n[the measured table](./providers#killableprocesses-across-the-bundled-providers))\nand no signal reaches the agent process: `kill()` does not stop it, and no\n`AbortSignal` crosses the provider boundary. There, **the sandbox destroy the\ncancel path performs is not cleanup, it is the cancel**. It is the only mechanism\nthat actually stops the agent. Wire a cancel to anything less (mark the record\n`'aborted'`, close the log, skip the destroy) and what you have built is\n\"marked cancelled, still running\": the UI says stopped while the agent keeps\nworking and the sandbox keeps billing.\n\n## Single-writer safety\n\nOnly one host may write a run, and the client has no safety\nnet below its offset de-dup. If two hosts both snapshot the log, both compute a\n\"remainder\", and both append it, the same logical chunk lands twice under two\ndifferent offsets, so it looks new to the client, and the stream processor\napplies text and tool-argument deltas unconditionally. You get doubled prose and\n`{\"a\":1}{\"a\":1}` tool arguments. Takeover is by definition two hosts wanting one\nrun, so the exclusion has to be real.\n\n`sandboxRunDriver` wires it for you, in three layers:\n\n1. **A lease.** The entire drive runs inside `LockStore.withLock` on a per-run\n   key, so the snapshot and every append after it are one critical section. A\n   lease-backed lock aborts the drive's signal the moment ownership is lost.\n2. **An epoch.** Each successful claim bumps `RunRecord.driverEpoch`. The log\n   re-reads it periodically and refuses to append once a higher epoch exists.\n   This covers what a lease cannot: a lock whose renewal is coarser than the\n   run's append rate, or one whose signal never fires at all.\n3. **Quiescence.** The successor waits for the stored log to stop growing before\n   its first append, so a predecessor that is still writing is *observed* rather\n   than raced. The window defaults to `DEFAULT_FENCE_QUIET_MS` (5 seconds) and is\n   configurable with `fenceQuietMs`. If the log never quiesces, the drive fails\n   instead of appending into a log another host is still writing.\n\n### Both authoritative channels are fenced\n\nA run's facts live in **two** places: its event log and its record. Fencing only\nthe log does not remove the harm, it relocates it. A superseded driver whose\nappend is refused folds that refusal into a terminal `runs.update`, and the\nrecord then reads `'failed'` for a run the successor is healthily streaming.\nEvery consumer that branches on terminal status (`isTerminalRunStatus`,\n`findActiveRun`, a status poller, a reaper) would then believe a live run had\ndied, on the authority of a host that no longer owns it.\n\nSo both seams are closed, over the same claim:\n\n- **The log.** A superseded driver's `append` is refused. The **first** refusal\n  latches the fence permanently shut: every later append refuses immediately,\n  without a re-read. That permanence matters, because the refused append is followed\n  directly by the recovery path's own terminal `RUN_ERROR`, and that log belongs\n  to the *successor*, so a dead host's error chunk would fail the stream for\n  every client attached to the healthy run.\n- **The record.** A terminal-status `update` from a lost claim is **suppressed**:\n  it resolves without writing. Non-terminal writes still pass through, including\n  a stale `detachedSince` or `sandboxKey`. Those cannot make a live run look\n  finished, and the successor owns and overwrites them anyway.\n\nThe practical upshot for you: **a terminal status on the record is trustworthy.**\nA status poller may believe it.\n\n`close()` is outside both fences, deliberately. It runs on every teardown path\nincluding the teardown *caused* by losing the claim, and a fenced `close` would\nwedge the record at `'running'` with every live tailer parked forever: a\ndurability `read` only ends when the log closes. A wedged run with parked clients\nis worse than the write being prevented.\n\n### Errors you can branch on\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  RunClaimLostError,\n  RunClaimNotAcquiredError,\n  RunDriverPipeOutsideClaimError,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nfunction describeDriveFailure(error: unknown): string {\n  if (error instanceof RunClaimNotAcquiredError) {\n    // 'terminal' | 'unknown' | 'superseded': normal, not a bug.\n    return `not driving ${error.runId}: ${error.reason}`\n  }\n  if (error instanceof RunClaimLostError) {\n    return `superseded mid-drive at epoch ${error.heldEpoch}`\n  }\n  if (error instanceof RunDriverPipeOutsideClaimError) {\n    // A programming error: the options object was taken apart and `pipe` called\n    // outside `claim`, so there is no epoch to fence with.\n    return `run ${error.runId}: pipe ran outside its claim`\n  }\n  throw error\n}\n```\n\nThe first two are ordinary outcomes of a contended takeover and\n`resumeServerSentEventsResponse` already swallows both. You will see them in\nlogs, not in responses.\n\n### What this is not\n\nIt is not airtight fencing. A predecessor paused (GC, VM suspend) for longer than\nthe quiescence window, between its last fence check and its append landing at the\nbackend, can still write one batch. Closing that needs a compare-and-set on the\ndurability write, which `StreamDurability.append` does not offer. The mitigation\nis deployment-level: use a lease-backed distributed `LockStore`, and keep\n`fenceQuietMs` above the lease's renewal interval.\n\n## Replay and divergence\n\nA takeover does not resume the journal from where the dead host stopped. It\nre-reads the journal **from byte zero** and re-translates it, which reproduces\nchunks the client already has. Alignment is what makes that safe: the stored log\nis read once, the replay is verified against it by fingerprint, the matching\nprefix is suppressed, and only the remainder is appended and delivered. The log\n*is* the checkpoint, so there is no window in which a checkpoint and the log can\ndisagree. The precedence rule this implements: **the log wins for what clients\nsee; the journal wins for where the taking-over driver resumes.**\n\nTwo properties make the comparison possible, and both are already true on the\njournaled path:\n\n- **Ids are deterministic.** A journaled run mints message ids from a run-scoped\n  counter (`<runId>-0`, `<runId>-1`, …) rather than a timestamp plus randomness.\n- **Alignment runs only on an attach.** On a fresh run the transform's premise\n  (\"this stream is a replay of what is already stored\") is false, and aligning\n  anyway would match a fresh run's own chunks against pre-existing log entries\n  and silently *suppress* them. That is data loss, not a slow path.\n\n### `JournalReplayDivergedError`\n\nIf the replay produces a different chunk than the log already holds at that\nindex, `JournalReplayDivergedError` is thrown with the index and both\nfingerprints.\n\n```ts\nimport { JournalReplayDivergedError } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nfunction report(error: unknown): string {\n  if (error instanceof JournalReplayDivergedError) {\n    return `diverged at ${error.index}: stored ${error.stored}, replayed ${error.replayed}`\n  }\n  throw error\n}\n```\n\nRead it plainly: **the agent's replay produced a different sequence of events\nthan the log already delivered.** Translation stopped being deterministic. The\nrealistic causes are an id generator that is not run-scoped, a translator that\nconsults the clock, or a journal that was rewritten (most often a reused\n`runId`).\n\nWhat to do about it: treat it as a bug to fix, not a condition to recover from.\nDo not catch it and continue. The log is authoritative and already went to the\nclient, so forwarding past a mismatch delivers a stream whose prefix and suffix\ndisagree about message identity, and the client cannot survive that. Log the\nindex and both fingerprints, let the run fail, and check `runId` uniqueness\nfirst.\n\nOne tolerance exists. On adapters that splice host-tool-bridge events into their\noutput (`@tanstack/ai-codex`, `@tanstack/ai-claude-code`), the log holds `CUSTOM`\nchunks fired by *live* tool execution, which a replay runs no tools to reproduce.\nAlignment skips those as out-of-band, up to `DEFAULT_MAX_OUT_OF_BAND_SKIP` (64)\nconsecutive entries. The bound is what keeps this a tolerance rather than a\nsearch: unbounded, a genuine determinism regression would scan forward looking\nfor any fingerprint that happens to match.\n\n## Configuration\n\nAll of these live under `withSandbox(sandbox, { durability: { … } })`.\n\n| Option | Default | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `adapter` | required | The run's delivery-durable event log. Same instance you hand the transport. |\n| `journal` | `/tmp/tanstack-runs` (`DEFAULT_JOURNAL_DIR`) | Journal directory inside the sandbox. |\n| `detachOnDisconnect` | `true` whenever durability is wired | Whether a disconnect detaches instead of destroying the sandbox. |\n| `attach` | `false` | Read an existing run's journal instead of starting an agent. Set by an attach route's `drive`, never by a `POST` handler. |\n| `pollIntervalMs` | adapter default | Journal poll interval for providers that cannot follow a file. |\n\nThere is deliberately no `detachedRunTtl` here. The only actor that enforces a\nTTL is `reapDetachedRuns`, which runs from a cron with no chat request in\nflight, so it cannot read anything `withSandbox` publishes on the\nper-request capability bus, so a TTL stored here could only ever go unread,\nwhile the sweep took its own `detachedRunTtlMs`, and the two would silently\ndisagree. So the sweep's `detachedRunTtlMs` (milliseconds, no default, no\nstring parsing) is the single source of truth; see [Reaping &\nRetention](./reaping) for sizing it.\n\n**The reaper ships, but nothing schedules it.** `reapDetachedRuns` from\n`@tanstack/ai-sandbox` is the sweep. It reads the optional\n`RunStore.listReclaimable({ now, ttlMs })`, which returns runs that are\n`'running'`, have a `detachedSince`, and whose `detachedSince <= now - ttlMs`\n(inclusive). It drives each run its out-of-band journal probe says already finished\nto a terminal status so the transcript lands, cancels and terminalizes the ones\npast `detachedRunTtlMs`, and destroys the sandbox named by `sandboxKey` through\n`sandboxReclaimer`. It never clears `detachedSince`: that marker is the evidence\nits TTL accounting selected the run on. (The takeover path clears it, because a\nviewer is attached again.)\n\nIt is a plain async function with no timer and no daemon, so **calling it on a\nschedule is your job**: a cron route, a queue consumer, a Durable Object\n`alarm()`, a `waitUntil`. Treat that as a hard requirement of wiring\n`durability`, not a nice-to-have: until something invokes it, nothing closes a\ndetached run's delivery log, so every attached client parks forever,\n`detachedRunTtlMs` is enforced by nothing, and the sandbox bills indefinitely.\n\n[Reaping & Retention](./reaping) is the whole picture: the sweep's outcomes, why\nit never drives a run to find out whether it finished, `pruneJournals`,\n`reclaimSandbox`, ready-to-paste schedules for Node, Vercel Cron, and a\nCloudflare `alarm()`, and how to size the TTL against the sweep interval.\n\nSet `detachOnDisconnect: false` to keep today's destroy-on-disconnect cost\nprofile while still getting resumable *delivery*: a reload replays the log, but\nthe agent does not survive the disconnect. An explicit cancel destroys the\nsandbox either way.\n\n### `DetachableRunCapability`\n\nA neutral boolean that core owns. `withSandbox` provides it as `true` only when a\nrun is genuinely durable; `withPersistence` reads it with `getOptional` to decide\nwhether an abort is terminal (`'aborted'`) or a detach (write nothing). It lives\nin core so the two packages can agree without either importing the other. A\npersistence → sandbox import would invert the layering. Read it in your own\nmiddleware if you need the same distinction; absent means \"not detachable\", which\nis every app that has not wired durability.\n\n### `RunDetachedCapability`\n\nIts past-tense counterpart, also owned by core. `DetachableRunCapability` is\npublished at setup and only says a disconnect *may* be survived;\n`RunDetachedCapability` is published on the abort path by `withSandbox`'s detach\nbranch and says the run **was** detached. The agent is still working and a later\nattach can take it over.\n\nIts consumer is the durable delivery sink behind `toServerSentEventsResponse` /\n`toHttpResponse`. Without the verdict the sink terminalizes every abort, which\ndefeats takeover (see the table above). The fact travels on the **stream object\nitself**, so there is nothing to wire: passing `chat()`'s stream to the response\nhelper is already mandatory, and both sides hold the same object.\n\n```ts\nimport { provideRunDetached } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { CapabilityContext } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// You do not write this. `withSandbox` does, on its detach branch, from a hook\n// that already holds the middleware context. Shown only so the fact is legible.\nfunction markRunDetached(ctx: CapabilityContext): void {\n  provideRunDetached(ctx, true)\n}\n```\n\nOnly a plain, intentless disconnect of a detachable run publishes it. An explicit\ncancel in either band, a disconnect on a non-detachable run,\n`detachOnDisconnect: false`, a provider failure, and a normal finish all leave it\nunpublished, and the sink appends its terminal and closes the log exactly as it\nalways has. Core additionally refuses to treat an abort carrying\n`RUN_CANCEL_REASON` as a detach, whatever a middleware publishes. A user pressing\nStop always gets a closed, terminal log.\n\n## Requirements\n\n**A real `LockStore`.** `InMemoryLockStore` cannot coordinate across hosts: it\nserializes claims within one process, and the signal it hands out is a fresh\n`AbortController().signal` that is never aborted, so the lease can never report a\nloss. Two replicas can then drive one run and duplicate its log. `withSandbox`\nemits a warning when durability is wired over an in-memory lock (including when\nno lock is wired at all, since the fallback *is* in-memory). See\n[Locks](../advanced/locks).\n\n**A caller-supplied `runId`.** A durable run throws\n`DurableRunIdRequiredError` at the start of `chatStream` when none is passed.\nThat is deliberately loud, because the alternative is invisible: an\nadapter-generated id produces a journal path no successor host can recompute, so\nthe run streams normally, records normally, and is silently unrecoverable. You\nwould only discover it during an incident.\n\n**The run record's `threadId`, on an attaching run.** An ATTACHING durable run\nthrows `DurableThreadIdRequiredError` when driven without the run record's\n`threadId`. `threadId` rides in **every** emitted chunk, so an attach that\ngenerates a fresh one replays a stream whose very first chunk already differs\nfrom the stored log. Alignment fails at index 0 with\n`JournalReplayThreadIdMismatchError`, a subclass of\n[`JournalReplayDivergedError`](#journalreplaydivergederror) reported above,\neven though the agent behaved identically. This is the asymmetry to keep\nstraight: a durable **fresh** run needs no caller `threadId`, because that run is\nwhat establishes it, but a durable run that is **attaching** must reuse the\none already on the record, which is exactly the `threadId: input.threadId`\nforwarded by `driveRun` above.\n\n**A `RunStore` that persists every durable-run field.** `createOrResume`,\n`update`, and `get` are required; `update` must accept and round-trip all of\n`status`, `finishedAt`, `error`, `usage`, `sandboxKey`, `detachedSince`,\n`cancelRequested`, and `driverEpoch`. The last four are exactly what a\nhand-written backend tends to omit, and each one breaks a specific\nmechanism: no `driverEpoch` means no fencing, no `cancelRequested` means Stop\ncannot reach a remote driver, no `detachedSince`/`sandboxKey` means nothing can\nreclaim the sandbox. Two invariants also hold: `createOrResume` returns an\nexisting record **unchanged** (that is what makes resuming safe), and `update` on\nan unknown `runId` is a **no-op** that must not throw.\n\n`findActiveRun` is required: it is how you rejoin by thread. `listReclaimable`\nis optional and feature-detected, but `reapDetachedRuns` needs it to have\nanything to sweep: a store without it cannot be reaped at all.\n\n## Adapter authors\n\nIf you are writing a harness adapter rather than an application, three exports\nare yours: `getSandboxDurability` reads the resolved durability off the\ncapability bus, `journalOptionsFor` turns it into the journal option\n`spawnNdjson` takes, and `alignedIfAttaching` applies alignment on attach only.\nWrap the *merged* output stream, never the pre-merge translator, or you compare\nagainst a stream the log never contained. See [Harnesses](./harnesses).\n\n## See also\n\n- [Durable Runs Explained](./durable-runs): the mental model behind this page, in\n  plain language and with no code. Start there if any of the above felt abrupt\n- [The Run Journal](./journal): where the agent's output lives, `runId`\n  uniqueness, and `alignToStoredLog`\n- [Sandbox Instance Durability](./durability): keeping the sandbox itself\n  findable across processes\n- [Lifecycle & Snapshots](./lifecycle): `destroyOnComplete`, `keepAlive`, and\n  snapshots\n- [Resumable Streams](../resumable-streams/overview) and\n  [Advanced](../resumable-streams/advanced): the delivery log, `joinRun`, and\n  offset ownership\n- [Locks](../advanced/locks): the distributed lock a durable run requires\n- [Persistence overview](../persistence/overview) and\n  [Store Reference](../persistence/store-reference): the shared\n  `RunStore`\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Takeover & Detached Runs (Advanced)\nid: sandbox-takeover\norder: 13\ndescription: \"Let a sandboxed agent run outlive the browser tab: detach on disconnect, then take the run over from another host and resume streaming where the log left off.\"\nkeywords:\n  - detached run\n  - run takeover\n  - sandboxRunDriver\n  - detachOnDisconnect\n  - detachedRunTtlMs\n  - requestRunCancel\n  - JournalReplayDivergedError\n  - driverEpoch\n---\n\nA sandboxed coding agent can work for ten minutes. A browser tab does not last\nten minutes. Users refresh, close the laptop, lose wifi, and hit a load balancer\nthat lands the next request on a different replica.\n\nWithout durability wired, the disconnect is fatal: `withSandbox`'s abort path\ndestroys the sandbox on every abort, deliberately, because closing the agent's\nIO stream does **not** kill the agent process (a Docker `exec` survives its\nclient), so destroying the container is the only reliable way to stop it burning\ntokens. Correct for a cancel. Ruinous for a refresh.\n\nDurable runs split those two cases apart. A disconnect **detaches**: the agent\nkeeps working, the sandbox stays up, and the run record remembers that nobody is\nwatching. A later request **takes the run over**, replays what was already\ndelivered, and continues streaming the rest.\n\nThis page is the wiring. It builds on two pages you should read first:\n[The Run Journal](./journal) (where the agent's output lives, and why it is a\nfile rather than a pipe) and\n[Resumable Streams](../resumable-streams/overview) (the delivery log a client\nreconnects to).\n\n## Durability is one opt-in, not two\n\n`withSandbox` takes `runs` and `durability`. A run is durable only when **both**\nare present, because either alone is useless: a record with no event log cannot\nbe replayed, and a log with no record cannot be found, claimed, or reaped. There\nis no half-configured state. Pass one and you get exactly today's behavior,\nsilently and with no warning, because you have not asked for durability.\n\nPass the **same** `RunStore` chat persistence uses, so one record describes the\nrun instead of two that disagree.\n\n## Both routes must address the same backend stream\n\nRead this before the snippets. A `StreamDurability` is bound to **one run**,\nand `durableStream(request, options)` resolves which run through core's\n`resolveResumeRunId`: the `X-Run-Id` header first, then `?runId` on the\nrequest URL. Core's own `memoryStream` uses the same resolver, so no two\ndurability adapters can disagree about which run a request names.\n\n- A **POST from `@tanstack/ai-client`** carries the run id in the `X-Run-Id`\n  header, not the URL. The POST URL stays byte-identical to a plain, non-durable\n  chat request.\n- A **GET join** carries it in the URL instead: `joinRun` requests\n  `?offset=-1&runId=<runId>`.\n\nSo `durableStream(request, durableOptions)` on both routes resolves the same\n`agent-runs/<runId>` stream, whichever way the run id arrived. Nothing to rewrite,\nnothing to force onto a copy of the URL:\n\n```ts\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\n\n// The external Durable Streams backend every replica can reach. See\n// ../resumable-streams/advanced for the full option set (auth headers, batch\n// size, reconnect tuning).\nexport const durableOptions = {\n  server: 'https://streams.example.com',\n  streamPrefix: 'agent-runs',\n}\n```\n\nA request naming no run at all (neither header nor query) throws rather than\nsilently producing into a stream no attach request could ever name:\n\n```\ndurableStream: a runId is required: send it as an X-Run-Id header or a\n?runId query param\n```\n\nThat only bites a client that bypasses `@tanstack/ai-client` (a custom\n`fetch`, a hand-rolled reconnect): make sure it sends one of the two, an\n`X-Run-Id` header on the POST or `?runId` on the URL. A mid-stream SSE\nreconnect to the POST route still works. It arrives carrying `Last-Event-ID` and the\nsame `X-Run-Id` header the original POST used, so `durableStream` resolves the run and\nhonors the resume offset.\n\nAnd a caller with no live `Request` at all (a cron, a Durable Object `alarm()`)\nsynthesizes one from scratch instead. See the reaper's own\n`durabilityFor` in [Reaping & Retention](./reaping).\n\n## Server: start a durable run\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { withLocks } from '@tanstack/ai/locks'\nimport { claudeCodeText } from '@tanstack/ai-claude-code'\nimport { memoryPersistence, withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\n// The options from the section above.\nimport { durableOptions } from './durability'\n// Your distributed LockStore. `InMemoryLockStore` is NOT enough here. See\n// \"Requirements\" below.\nimport { locks } from './locks'\n// Your `defineSandbox(...)` result.\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\n\n// Development stand-in. A durable run needs a store every replica can read;\n// see ../persistence/build-your-own-adapter.\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\nconst { runs } = persistence.stores\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  // ONE adapter instance, handed to both the middleware and the transport, so\n  // the journal path and the delivery log describe the same run.\n  // `@tanstack/ai-client` sends `X-Run-Id` on every POST, which is exactly what\n  // `durableStream` resolves first, so this already addresses\n  // `agent-runs/<runId>`: the same stream the attach route below reads.\n  const adapter = durableStream(request, durableOptions)\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: claudeCodeText('claude-opus-4-8'),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    // Required for a durable run. Omit it and `chatStream` throws\n    // `DurableRunIdRequiredError`. See \"Requirements\".\n    runId,\n    middleware: [\n      withPersistence(persistence),\n      withLocks(locks),\n      withSandbox(sandbox, {\n        runs,\n        durability: { adapter },\n      }),\n    ],\n  })\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, { durability: { adapter } })\n}\n```\n\nThree things changed compared with a plain sandboxed chat endpoint, and each one\nis load bearing:\n\n- **`runs` + `durability`** turn on detach-on-disconnect and publish\n  `DetachableRunCapability` on the bus, which is how `withPersistence` learns\n  that an abort on this run is a detach rather than a cancel.\n- **`runId` is forwarded**, not generated. The journal path, the deterministic\n  message-id generator, **and the backend stream name** are all derived from it,\n  so a successor host can only resume a run whose `runId` it can recompute.\n- **The adapter instance is shared** between `withSandbox` and the response, and it is\n  keyed by that same `runId`. That is what makes the attach route below address this\n  very stream.\n\nOne thing must **not** be there: an `abortController` that mirrors `request.signal`.\n\nA plain sandboxed endpoint mirrors it, because there a disconnect should end the run.\nOn a durable run it destroys the thing you are trying to protect. Aborting the run\nmakes `chat()` return at its cancellation check right after middleware `setup`, so the\nharness adapter's `chatStream` is never called and **the agent inside the sandbox you\njust spent minutes building is never launched**. Switch tabs while the UI still says\n\"starting the sandbox\" and you come back to an empty log for a run that did nothing.\nNo takeover can recover it either, because an agent that never started wrote no\njournal to replay.\n\nThe disconnect still reaches `withSandbox` without it. The durable transport notifies\nthe run the moment the response body is cancelled, **without** aborting it, and then:\n\n- `withSandbox` stamps `detachedSince` and `sandboxKey`, and publishes the detach\n  verdict.\n- The run keeps draining into its still-open delivery log.\n- A rejoining client tails that log.\n\nPassing `runs` plus `durability` buys all of that. There is nothing else to wire.\n\nA genuine stop is unaffected, because it arrives out of band (see\n[Detach vs cancel](#detach-vs-cancel)). That is the only channel that can tell \"the\nuser wants this stopped\" from \"the user closed a tab\", since both are the same socket\nclose on the wire.\n\n### On `memoryStream`, raise the first-chunk deadline\n\nA rejoin during the sandbox build fails with\n`Memory stream run produced no data within 100ms`, and the run it calls gone is\nhealthy. `memoryStream` gives up on a from-start rejoin when the run produces no chunk\nwithin `firstChunkDeadlineMs`, which defaults to 100ms. That default fits chat, where\nan in-flight run's log already holds chunks. A sandboxed run emits nothing until\n`ensure` has built the sandbox and cloned the repo.\n\nRaise it on **every** handle for the run. The `GET` that serves the rejoin is the call\nthat actually applies it:\n\n```ts\nimport { memoryStream } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\n// Longer than your slowest `ensure`.\nconst FIRST_CHUNK_DEADLINE_MS = 15 * 60_000;\n\n// POST (the producer) and GET (the rejoin) alike.\nexport function adapterFor(request: Request) {\n  return memoryStream(request, {\n    firstChunkDeadlineMs: FIRST_CHUNK_DEADLINE_MS,\n  });\n}\n```\n\nFailing fast buys nothing once you gate the rejoin on `findActiveRun`, which already\nexcludes a run that is really gone before the join is attempted.\n\nTwo things make this a backstop rather than the main defence:\n\n- `durableStream` runs no first-chunk deadline at all. Its `read` parks for a live\n  reader, so an empty in-flight log simply waits.\n- A fresh durable producer appends one `CUSTOM` `run.accepted` chunk\n  (`RUN_ACCEPTED_EVENT`) before it pulls the producer stream, so a rejoin attaches in\n  milliseconds instead of waiting on the harness. That chunk also keeps the client from\n  abandoning the join, since `ai-client` gives up on a rejoin that receives nothing for\n  2s and a sandbox build always takes longer.\n\n## Server: take the run over\n\nThe takeover happens in the `GET` handler that already serves resumes. Add a\n`driver` and the same request that replays the log also claims the run and keeps\ndriving it.\n\nEvery adapter here comes from the **same** `durableStream(request, durableOptions)`\nthe `POST` route used, so the log this route replays, the log the drive appends\nto, and the log the producing route wrote are provably one stream:\n`agent-runs/<runId>`.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, resumeServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { withLocks } from '@tanstack/ai/locks'\nimport { claudeCodeText } from '@tanstack/ai-claude-code'\nimport { durableStream } from '@tanstack/ai-durable-stream'\nimport { memoryPersistence, withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { sandboxRunDriver, withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n// The same backend options as the POST route.\nimport { durableOptions } from './durability'\nimport { locks } from './locks'\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\nimport type { StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\nconst { messages: messageStore, runs } = persistence.stores\n\n/**\n * The claim hands `drive` an `AbortSignal` that fires the moment this host loses\n * ownership; `chat()` takes an `AbortController`. Mirror one onto the other so a\n * lost claim actually stops the drive.\n */\nfunction controllerFor(signal: AbortSignal): AbortController {\n  const controller = new AbortController()\n  const abort = (): void => controller.abort(signal.reason)\n  if (signal.aborted) abort()\n  else signal.addEventListener('abort', abort, { once: true })\n  return controller\n}\n\nexport function GET(request: Request) {\n  async function* driveRun(input: {\n    runId: string\n    threadId: string\n    signal: AbortSignal\n  }): AsyncIterable<StreamChunk> {\n    // The client sent no history: it is reconnecting, not asking a question.\n    const stored = await messageStore.loadThread(input.threadId)\n    const stream = chat({\n      adapter: claudeCodeText('claude-opus-4-8'),\n      messages: stored,\n      threadId: input.threadId,\n      runId: input.runId,\n      abortController: controllerFor(input.signal),\n      middleware: [\n        withPersistence(persistence),\n        withLocks(locks),\n        withSandbox(sandbox, {\n          runs,\n          // `attach: true` is the whole difference: the harness tails the run's\n          // EXISTING journal instead of starting a second agent. It belongs\n          // here and never on `chat()`, which has no sandbox vocabulary. It\n          // is set only by an attach route, never by a POST handler.\n          durability: {\n            // `request` already names this run (`?runId` on an attach GET),\n            // so `durableStream` resolves the same `agent-runs/<runId>` the\n            // journal replay aligns against.\n            adapter: durableStream(request, durableOptions),\n            attach: true,\n          },\n        }),\n      ],\n    })\n    for await (const chunk of stream) yield chunk\n  }\n\n  return resumeServerSentEventsResponse({\n    // The replaying adapter is the one adapter here whose `resumeFrom()` matters,\n    // and the offset it must return (`?offset=-1` from a join, or an SSE\n    // reconnect's `Last-Event-ID`) lives on the incoming request. `durableStream`\n    // resolves the run the same way on every route, `X-Run-Id` header first,\n    // then `?runId`, so this addresses the same `agent-runs/<runId>` the POST\n    // route wrote, whichever way a given request names the run.\n    adapter: durableStream(request, durableOptions),\n    driver: sandboxRunDriver({\n      request,\n      runs,\n      locks,\n      // Per-run log factory. Core resolves the id from this same request\n      // through the same `resolveResumeRunId` `durableStream` uses, so every\n      // call for this run talks to the same backend stream and `snapshot()`\n      // sees this host's own appends. The state lives in the Durable Streams\n      // backend, not this process.\n      durability: () => durableStream(request, durableOptions),\n      drive: driveRun,\n    }),\n  })\n}\n```\n\nThe response is **byte-identical** whether or not you pass `driver`: it still\nreplays from the durability log. The drive runs beside it, appending to the run's\nproducer-side log, and the response tails what lands. That separation is what\nlets a taken-over run keep `chat()`'s normal middleware path, so\n`withPersistence`'s `onFinish` still saves the transcript of a run that finished\nwhile detached.\n\nEverything about the takeover is **total by construction**. Every failure is\nlogged and swallowed, and the response still serves the log:\n\n| Situation | What happens |\n| --- | --- |\n| No run id on the request, or no record | Serve the log, drive nothing. |\n| The record is already terminal | Serve the log, drive nothing. A second tab attaching to a finished run must still see the transcript. |\n| Another host holds the claim | Serve the log, drive nothing. Two tabs attaching at once: one wins the lease and drives, the other tails. |\n| The drive throws | Logged server-side. It cannot be reported to a response that is already streaming; the run's own `RUN_ERROR` event is that channel. |\n\nServerless platforms need a keep-alive for the background drive. Pass\n`waitUntil: (promise) => ctx.waitUntil(promise)`.\n\n## Client: reconnect and continue\n\nA mid-stream drop needs nothing: `useChat` reconnects with the last offset and\nthe server replays from the log.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const chat = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <button onClick={() => void chat.sendMessage('Refactor the auth module')}>\n      Send ({chat.messages.length} messages)\n    </button>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nA full reload is different. The page comes back with no `Last-Event-ID`, so it\nhas to ask which run is still going and then join it from the start with\n`joinRun` (a read-only `GET` with `offset=-1`), which is exactly the handler\nabove. That `GET` is what claims the run, so joining and taking over are the same\nrequest.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\nimport { useEffect, useState } from 'react'\nimport type { StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nexport function ResumeInFlight({ threadId }: { threadId: string }) {\n  const [chunks, setChunks] = useState<Array<StreamChunk>>([])\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const controller = new AbortController()\n    const connection = fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat')\n\n    async function rejoin(): Promise<void> {\n      const response = await fetch(\n        `/api/chat/active?threadId=${encodeURIComponent(threadId)}`,\n        { signal: controller.signal },\n      )\n      const body: unknown = await response.json()\n      if (typeof body !== 'object' || body === null || !('runId' in body)) return\n      const runId = body.runId\n      if (typeof runId !== 'string') return\n      for await (const chunk of connection.joinRun(runId, controller.signal)) {\n        setChunks((previous) => [...previous, chunk])\n      }\n    }\n\n    void rejoin().catch(() => {\n      // The run finished, or there was none. Nothing to rejoin.\n    })\n    return () => controller.abort()\n  }, [threadId])\n\n  return <p>{chunks.length} events replayed</p>\n}\n```\n\nThe \"which run\" endpoint is `RunStore.findActiveRun`, which resolves a live run\nfrom the **stable** `threadId` rather than the ephemeral run id a single turn may\nmint several of. It is an optional store method, so feature-detect it.\n\n```ts\nimport { memoryPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\nconst { runs } = persistence.stores\n\nexport async function GET(request: Request) {\n  const threadId = new URL(request.url).searchParams.get('threadId')\n  if (threadId === null) {\n    return new Response('threadId is required', { status: 400 })\n  }\n  // Optional on the RunStore contract, so a backend that omits it degrades to\n  // \"no active run\" instead of throwing.\n  const active = await runs.findActiveRun(threadId)\n  return Response.json({ runId: active?.runId ?? null })\n}\n```\n\nAll four HTTP connection adapters (`fetchServerSentEvents`, `fetchHttpStream`,\n`xhrServerSentEvents`, `xhrHttpStream`) expose `joinRun`. For NDJSON, pair it\nwith `resumeHttpResponse` on the server; the driver wiring is identical.\n\n## Detach vs cancel\n\n**This is the part that is easy to get wrong, and getting it wrong is\nexpensive.** A user pressing Stop and a user closing the tab produce the\n*identical* connection close. There is nothing in the disconnect to tell them\napart.\n\nSo intent is never inferred from the disconnect. It arrives **out of band**, and\nthere are exactly two bands, and either one is authoritative:\n\n1. **Durable**: `requestRunCancel(runs, runId)` records `cancelRequested` on\n   the run record. This is the only channel that reaches a run being driven by a\n   *different* host than the one the cancel request landed on, which is the\n   normal case for a detached run.\n2. **In-process**: abort the run's own `AbortController` with\n   `RUN_CANCEL_REASON`. Core reads that reason back when it builds `AbortInfo`,\n   so `AbortInfo.cancelRequested` is `true` for that abort and `false` for a\n   plain disconnect. Fast path when the cancel reaches the driving host.\n\nA cancel endpoint should do **both**. `requestRunCancel` deliberately writes no\nstatus: recording intent is not the same as the run having stopped, and only the\ndriver knows when the agent is actually dead and the sandbox is gone.\n\n```ts\nimport { RUN_CANCEL_REASON, requestRunCancel } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { memoryPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\nconst { runs } = persistence.stores\n\n/**\n * Runs this process is currently driving. Only ever a fast path: a run driven by\n * another replica is absent here, and the durable band is what reaches it.\n */\nconst driving = new Map<string, AbortController>()\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const body: unknown = await request.json()\n  if (typeof body !== 'object' || body === null || !('threadId' in body)) {\n    return new Response('threadId is required', { status: 400 })\n  }\n  const threadId = body.threadId\n  if (typeof threadId !== 'string') {\n    return new Response('threadId must be a string', { status: 400 })\n  }\n\n  const active = await runs.findActiveRun(threadId)\n  if (!active) return new Response(null, { status: 204 })\n\n  // Band 1: durable, so a remote driver observes it on its next teardown.\n  await requestRunCancel(runs, active.runId)\n  // Band 2: in-process, so a co-located driver stops immediately.\n  driving.get(active.runId)?.abort(RUN_CANCEL_REASON)\n\n  return new Response(null, { status: 204 })\n}\n```\n\nPopulate `driving` where you create the run's `AbortController`: in the `POST`\nhandler for a fresh run, and in `controllerFor` on the takeover path.\n\nOn the client, `chat.stop()` sends the server nothing. A durable run then\ncontinues, because a refresh looks the same. Call the endpoint too.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function StoppableChat({ threadId }: { threadId: string }) {\n  const chat = useChat({\n    threadId,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  })\n\n  async function stopRun(): Promise<void> {\n    // Local: stop rendering the stream immediately.\n    chat.stop()\n    // Remote: tell the server this was intent, not a lost connection.\n    await fetch('/api/chat/cancel', {\n      method: 'POST',\n      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n      body: JSON.stringify({ threadId }),\n    })\n  }\n\n  return <button onClick={() => void stopRun()}>Stop</button>\n}\n```\n\n### What each path writes\n\n| Event | `withSandbox` | `withPersistence` | Delivery log |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Disconnect, durable, `detachOnDisconnect` on, no cancel recorded | Keeps the sandbox; writes `detachedSince` and `sandboxKey` | Writes **nothing**; the record stays `'running'` | Left **open**, no terminal appended |\n| Cancel (either band) | Destroys the sandbox, always, regardless of `destroyOnComplete` | Writes `'aborted'` with `finishedAt` | Terminal `RUN_ERROR`, then closed |\n| Disconnect on a non-durable run | Destroys the sandbox | Writes `'aborted'` | Terminal `RUN_ERROR`, then closed |\n\nThe delivery-log column is what makes takeover work at all. A detached run's log\nhas to stay open and terminal-free: a closed log ends the attaching client's\nreplay at the prefix, and a stored synthetic `RUN_ERROR` is a chunk the takeover's\njournal replay cannot reproduce, so alignment diverges and a perfectly healthy run\nis recorded as `'failed'`. See [`RunDetachedCapability`](#rundetachedcapability)\nfor how the verdict reaches the transport.\n\n`keepAlive` / `destroyOnComplete: false` govern *successful completion* only.\nThey never keep a sandbox alive through a cancel.\n\nGet this backwards in either direction and it hurts: a Stop button that only\ncalls `chat.stop()` leaks running sandboxes that keep spending tokens with nobody\nwatching, and treating every disconnect as a cancel kills a user's ten-minute\nrefactor on a wifi blip.\n\n### What cancel means on a provider that cannot kill\n\nOn a provider whose capabilities declare `killableProcesses: false` (Daytona,\nVercel, and Cloudflare among the bundled ones (see\n[the measured table](./providers#killableprocesses-across-the-bundled-providers))\nand no signal reaches the agent process: `kill()` does not stop it, and no\n`AbortSignal` crosses the provider boundary. There, **the sandbox destroy the\ncancel path performs is not cleanup, it is the cancel**. It is the only mechanism\nthat actually stops the agent. Wire a cancel to anything less (mark the record\n`'aborted'`, close the log, skip the destroy) and what you have built is\n\"marked cancelled, still running\": the UI says stopped while the agent keeps\nworking and the sandbox keeps billing.\n\n## Single-writer safety\n\nOnly one host may write a run, and the client has no safety\nnet below its offset de-dup. If two hosts both snapshot the log, both compute a\n\"remainder\", and both append it, the same logical chunk lands twice under two\ndifferent offsets, so it looks new to the client, and the stream processor\napplies text and tool-argument deltas unconditionally. You get doubled prose and\n`{\"a\":1}{\"a\":1}` tool arguments. Takeover is by definition two hosts wanting one\nrun, so the exclusion has to be real.\n\n`sandboxRunDriver` wires it for you, in three layers:\n\n1. **A lease.** The entire drive runs inside `LockStore.withLock` on a per-run\n   key, so the snapshot and every append after it are one critical section. A\n   lease-backed lock aborts the drive's signal the moment ownership is lost.\n2. **An epoch.** Each successful claim bumps `RunRecord.driverEpoch`. The log\n   re-reads it periodically and refuses to append once a higher epoch exists.\n   This covers what a lease cannot: a lock whose renewal is coarser than the\n   run's append rate, or one whose signal never fires at all.\n3. **Quiescence.** The successor waits for the stored log to stop growing before\n   its first append, so a predecessor that is still writing is *observed* rather\n   than raced. The window defaults to `DEFAULT_FENCE_QUIET_MS` (5 seconds) and is\n   configurable with `fenceQuietMs`. If the log never quiesces, the drive fails\n   instead of appending into a log another host is still writing.\n\n### Both authoritative channels are fenced\n\nA run's facts live in **two** places: its event log and its record. Fencing only\nthe log does not remove the harm, it relocates it. A superseded driver whose\nappend is refused folds that refusal into a terminal `runs.update`, and the\nrecord then reads `'failed'` for a run the successor is healthily streaming.\nEvery consumer that branches on terminal status (`isTerminalRunStatus`,\n`findActiveRun`, a status poller, a reaper) would then believe a live run had\ndied, on the authority of a host that no longer owns it.\n\nSo both seams are closed, over the same claim:\n\n- **The log.** A superseded driver's `append` is refused. The **first** refusal\n  latches the fence permanently shut: every later append refuses immediately,\n  without a re-read. That permanence matters, because the refused append is followed\n  directly by the recovery path's own terminal `RUN_ERROR`, and that log belongs\n  to the *successor*, so a dead host's error chunk would fail the stream for\n  every client attached to the healthy run.\n- **The record.** A terminal-status `update` from a lost claim is **suppressed**:\n  it resolves without writing. Non-terminal writes still pass through, including\n  a stale `detachedSince` or `sandboxKey`. Those cannot make a live run look\n  finished, and the successor owns and overwrites them anyway.\n\nThe practical upshot for you: **a terminal status on the record is trustworthy.**\nA status poller may believe it.\n\n`close()` is outside both fences, deliberately. It runs on every teardown path\nincluding the teardown *caused* by losing the claim, and a fenced `close` would\nwedge the record at `'running'` with every live tailer parked forever: a\ndurability `read` only ends when the log closes. A wedged run with parked clients\nis worse than the write being prevented.\n\n### Errors you can branch on\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  RunClaimLostError,\n  RunClaimNotAcquiredError,\n  RunDriverPipeOutsideClaimError,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nfunction describeDriveFailure(error: unknown): string {\n  if (error instanceof RunClaimNotAcquiredError) {\n    // 'terminal' | 'unknown' | 'superseded': normal, not a bug.\n    return `not driving ${error.runId}: ${error.reason}`\n  }\n  if (error instanceof RunClaimLostError) {\n    return `superseded mid-drive at epoch ${error.heldEpoch}`\n  }\n  if (error instanceof RunDriverPipeOutsideClaimError) {\n    // A programming error: the options object was taken apart and `pipe` called\n    // outside `claim`, so there is no epoch to fence with.\n    return `run ${error.runId}: pipe ran outside its claim`\n  }\n  throw error\n}\n```\n\nThe first two are ordinary outcomes of a contended takeover and\n`resumeServerSentEventsResponse` already swallows both. You will see them in\nlogs, not in responses.\n\n### What this is not\n\nIt is not airtight fencing. A predecessor paused (GC, VM suspend) for longer than\nthe quiescence window, between its last fence check and its append landing at the\nbackend, can still write one batch. Closing that needs a compare-and-set on the\ndurability write, which `StreamDurability.append` does not offer. The mitigation\nis deployment-level: use a lease-backed distributed `LockStore`, and keep\n`fenceQuietMs` above the lease's renewal interval.\n\n## Replay and divergence\n\nA takeover does not resume the journal from where the dead host stopped. It\nre-reads the journal **from byte zero** and re-translates it, which reproduces\nchunks the client already has. Alignment is what makes that safe: the stored log\nis read once, the replay is verified against it by fingerprint, the matching\nprefix is suppressed, and only the remainder is appended and delivered. The log\n*is* the checkpoint, so there is no window in which a checkpoint and the log can\ndisagree. The precedence rule this implements: **the log wins for what clients\nsee; the journal wins for where the taking-over driver resumes.**\n\nTwo properties make the comparison possible, and both are already true on the\njournaled path:\n\n- **Ids are deterministic.** A journaled run mints message ids from a run-scoped\n  counter (`<runId>-0`, `<runId>-1`, …) rather than a timestamp plus randomness.\n- **Alignment runs only on an attach.** On a fresh run the transform's premise\n  (\"this stream is a replay of what is already stored\") is false, and aligning\n  anyway would match a fresh run's own chunks against pre-existing log entries\n  and silently *suppress* them. That is data loss, not a slow path.\n\n### `JournalReplayDivergedError`\n\nIf the replay produces a different chunk than the log already holds at that\nindex, `JournalReplayDivergedError` is thrown with the index and both\nfingerprints.\n\n```ts\nimport { JournalReplayDivergedError } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nfunction report(error: unknown): string {\n  if (error instanceof JournalReplayDivergedError) {\n    return `diverged at ${error.index}: stored ${error.stored}, replayed ${error.replayed}`\n  }\n  throw error\n}\n```\n\nRead it plainly: **the agent's replay produced a different sequence of events\nthan the log already delivered.** Translation stopped being deterministic. The\nrealistic causes are an id generator that is not run-scoped, a translator that\nconsults the clock, or a journal that was rewritten (most often a reused\n`runId`).\n\nWhat to do about it: treat it as a bug to fix, not a condition to recover from.\nDo not catch it and continue. The log is authoritative and already went to the\nclient, so forwarding past a mismatch delivers a stream whose prefix and suffix\ndisagree about message identity, and the client cannot survive that. Log the\nindex and both fingerprints, let the run fail, and check `runId` uniqueness\nfirst.\n\nOne tolerance exists. On adapters that splice host-tool-bridge events into their\noutput (`@tanstack/ai-codex`, `@tanstack/ai-claude-code`), the log holds `CUSTOM`\nchunks fired by *live* tool execution, which a replay runs no tools to reproduce.\nAlignment skips those as out-of-band, up to `DEFAULT_MAX_OUT_OF_BAND_SKIP` (64)\nconsecutive entries. The bound is what keeps this a tolerance rather than a\nsearch: unbounded, a genuine determinism regression would scan forward looking\nfor any fingerprint that happens to match.\n\n## Configuration\n\nAll of these live under `withSandbox(sandbox, { durability: { … } })`.\n\n| Option | Default | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `adapter` | required | The run's delivery-durable event log. Same instance you hand the transport. |\n| `journal` | `/tmp/tanstack-runs` (`DEFAULT_JOURNAL_DIR`) | Journal directory inside the sandbox. |\n| `detachOnDisconnect` | `true` whenever durability is wired | Whether a disconnect detaches instead of destroying the sandbox. |\n| `attach` | `false` | Read an existing run's journal instead of starting an agent. Set by an attach route's `drive`, never by a `POST` handler. |\n| `pollIntervalMs` | adapter default | Journal poll interval for providers that cannot follow a file. |\n\nThere is deliberately no `detachedRunTtl` here. The only actor that enforces a\nTTL is `reapDetachedRuns`, which runs from a cron with no chat request in\nflight, so it cannot read anything `withSandbox` publishes on the\nper-request capability bus, so a TTL stored here could only ever go unread,\nwhile the sweep took its own `detachedRunTtlMs`, and the two would silently\ndisagree. So the sweep's `detachedRunTtlMs` (milliseconds, no default, no\nstring parsing) is the single source of truth; see [Reaping &\nRetention](./reaping) for sizing it.\n\n**The reaper ships, but nothing schedules it.** `reapDetachedRuns` from\n`@tanstack/ai-sandbox` is the sweep. It reads the optional\n`RunStore.listReclaimable({ now, ttlMs })`, which returns runs that are\n`'running'`, have a `detachedSince`, and whose `detachedSince <= now - ttlMs`\n(inclusive). It drives each run its out-of-band journal probe says already finished\nto a terminal status so the transcript lands, cancels and terminalizes the ones\npast `detachedRunTtlMs`, and destroys the sandbox named by `sandboxKey` through\n`sandboxReclaimer`. It never clears `detachedSince`: that marker is the evidence\nits TTL accounting selected the run on. (The takeover path clears it, because a\nviewer is attached again.)\n\nIt is a plain async function with no timer and no daemon, so **calling it on a\nschedule is your job**: a cron route, a queue consumer, a Durable Object\n`alarm()`, a `waitUntil`. Treat that as a hard requirement of wiring\n`durability`, not a nice-to-have: until something invokes it, nothing closes a\ndetached run's delivery log, so every attached client parks forever,\n`detachedRunTtlMs` is enforced by nothing, and the sandbox bills indefinitely.\n\n[Reaping & Retention](./reaping) is the whole picture: the sweep's outcomes, why\nit never drives a run to find out whether it finished, `pruneJournals`,\n`reclaimSandbox`, ready-to-paste schedules for Node, Vercel Cron, and a\nCloudflare `alarm()`, and how to size the TTL against the sweep interval.\n\nSet `detachOnDisconnect: false` to keep today's destroy-on-disconnect cost\nprofile while still getting resumable *delivery*: a reload replays the log, but\nthe agent does not survive the disconnect. An explicit cancel destroys the\nsandbox either way.\n\n### `DetachableRunCapability`\n\nA neutral boolean that core owns. `withSandbox` provides it as `true` only when a\nrun is genuinely durable; `withPersistence` reads it with `getOptional` to decide\nwhether an abort is terminal (`'aborted'`) or a detach (write nothing). It lives\nin core so the two packages can agree without either importing the other. A\npersistence → sandbox import would invert the layering. Read it in your own\nmiddleware if you need the same distinction; absent means \"not detachable\", which\nis every app that has not wired durability.\n\n### `RunDetachedCapability`\n\nIts past-tense counterpart, also owned by core. `DetachableRunCapability` is\npublished at setup and only says a disconnect *may* be survived;\n`RunDetachedCapability` is published on the abort path by `withSandbox`'s detach\nbranch and says the run **was** detached. The agent is still working and a later\nattach can take it over.\n\nIts consumer is the durable delivery sink behind `toServerSentEventsResponse` /\n`toHttpResponse`. Without the verdict the sink terminalizes every abort, which\ndefeats takeover (see the table above). The fact travels on the **stream object\nitself**, so there is nothing to wire: passing `chat()`'s stream to the response\nhelper is already mandatory, and both sides hold the same object.\n\n```ts\nimport { provideRunDetached } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport type { CapabilityContext } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// You do not write this. `withSandbox` does, on its detach branch, from a hook\n// that already holds the middleware context. Shown only so the fact is legible.\nfunction markRunDetached(ctx: CapabilityContext): void {\n  provideRunDetached(ctx, true)\n}\n```\n\nOnly a plain, intentless disconnect of a detachable run publishes it. An explicit\ncancel in either band, a disconnect on a non-detachable run,\n`detachOnDisconnect: false`, a provider failure, and a normal finish all leave it\nunpublished, and the sink appends its terminal and closes the log exactly as it\nalways has. Core additionally refuses to treat an abort carrying\n`RUN_CANCEL_REASON` as a detach, whatever a middleware publishes. A user pressing\nStop always gets a closed, terminal log.\n\n## Requirements\n\n**A real `LockStore`.** `InMemoryLockStore` cannot coordinate across hosts: it\nserializes claims within one process, and the signal it hands out is a fresh\n`AbortController().signal` that is never aborted, so the lease can never report a\nloss. Two replicas can then drive one run and duplicate its log. `withSandbox`\nemits a warning when durability is wired over an in-memory lock (including when\nno lock is wired at all, since the fallback *is* in-memory). See\n[Locks](../advanced/locks).\n\n**A caller-supplied `runId`.** A durable run throws\n`DurableRunIdRequiredError` at the start of `chatStream` when none is passed.\nThat is deliberately loud, because the alternative is invisible: an\nadapter-generated id produces a journal path no successor host can recompute, so\nthe run streams normally, records normally, and is silently unrecoverable. You\nwould only discover it during an incident.\n\n**The run record's `threadId`, on an attaching run.** An ATTACHING durable run\nthrows `DurableThreadIdRequiredError` when driven without the run record's\n`threadId`. `threadId` rides in **every** emitted chunk, so an attach that\ngenerates a fresh one replays a stream whose very first chunk already differs\nfrom the stored log. Alignment fails at index 0 with\n`JournalReplayThreadIdMismatchError`, a subclass of\n[`JournalReplayDivergedError`](#journalreplaydivergederror) reported above,\neven though the agent behaved identically. This is the asymmetry to keep\nstraight: a durable **fresh** run needs no caller `threadId`, because that run is\nwhat establishes it, but a durable run that is **attaching** must reuse the\none already on the record, which is exactly the `threadId: input.threadId`\nforwarded by `driveRun` above.\n\n**A `RunStore` that persists every durable-run field.** `createOrResume`,\n`update`, and `get` are required; `update` must accept and round-trip all of\n`status`, `finishedAt`, `error`, `usage`, `sandboxKey`, `detachedSince`,\n`cancelRequested`, and `driverEpoch`. The last four are exactly what a\nhand-written backend tends to omit, and each one breaks a specific\nmechanism: no `driverEpoch` means no fencing, no `cancelRequested` means Stop\ncannot reach a remote driver, no `detachedSince`/`sandboxKey` means nothing can\nreclaim the sandbox. Two invariants also hold: `createOrResume` returns an\nexisting record **unchanged** (that is what makes resuming safe), and `update` on\nan unknown `runId` is a **no-op** that must not throw.\n\n`findActiveRun` is required: it is how you rejoin by thread. `listReclaimable`\nis optional and feature-detected, but `reapDetachedRuns` needs it to have\nanything to sweep: a store without it cannot be reaped at all.\n\n## Adapter authors\n\nIf you are writing a harness adapter rather than an application, three exports\nare yours: `getSandboxDurability` reads the resolved durability off the\ncapability bus, `journalOptionsFor` turns it into the journal option\n`spawnNdjson` takes, and `alignedIfAttaching` applies alignment on attach only.\nWrap the *merged* output stream, never the pre-merge translator, or you compare\nagainst a stream the log never contained. See [Harnesses](./harnesses).\n\n## See also\n\n- [Durable Runs Explained](./durable-runs): the mental model behind this page, in\n  plain language and with no code. Start there if any of the above felt abrupt\n- [The Run Journal](./journal): where the agent's output lives, `runId`\n  uniqueness, and `alignToStoredLog`\n- [Sandbox Instance Durability](./durability): keeping the sandbox itself\n  findable across processes\n- [Lifecycle & Snapshots](./lifecycle): `destroyOnComplete`, `keepAlive`, and\n  snapshots\n- [Resumable Streams](../resumable-streams/overview) and\n  [Advanced](../resumable-streams/advanced): the delivery log, `joinRun`, and\n  offset ownership\n- [Locks](../advanced/locks): the distributed lock a durable run requires\n- [Persistence overview](../persistence/overview) and\n  [Store Reference](../persistence/store-reference): the shared\n  `RunStore`\n"}
